600:
** ἀποκαθίστημι**,
_future_ -καταστήσω: _aorist_ ἀποκατέστησα, later ἀπεκατέστησα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 413.4 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}: _perfect_ **-καθέστᾰκα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.11.9, “SIG” 798.7 (1st c.AD)}}:—**re-establish, restore, reinstate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 6.3}}; τὰν πολιτείαν Canon Laws texts Byzantine cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.90; πολίτας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 7; συνθήκας εἰς τὸ ἐξ ἀρχῆς 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.23}}; ἀ. τινί τι **restore, return** it **to** one, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.98.7, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.65, etc.}}; ἀ. εἰς αὐτάν (i.e. φύσιν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 100c, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1204b37; εἰς τὸ αὐτό [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1074a3}}; εἰς ἀκέραιον, = **restituere in integrum**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“CIL” 1.203; τινὰ ἐς οἶκον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.27.6, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 7.6}}; ἀ. σαυτὸν εἰς ἐκεῖνον τὸν χρόνον **carry** yourself **back..,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.610d}}; ἐπί.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.23}}; **cure,** δασυσμοὺς φωνῆς{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.64, etc.}}
**2** **pay** what is due, ἀργύριον{{LXX|LXX.Gen.23.16.}}
**3** **hand over, deliver,** τοῖς φυλακίταις τὸ σῶμα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.359.9 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** in drill, **restore** a formation, etc., εἰς ὀρθὸν ἀ., ={εἰς ὀρθὸν ἀποδοῦναι}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 26.3 (compare ἀποδίδωμι 11.3); ἀποκατάστησον}}**as you were!** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 12.11, etc.}}
**II** _passive_, with _perfect_ ἀποκαθέστᾰμαι, _aorist_ ἀποκαθεστάθην [ᾰ]: also _aorist 2_ _active_ -κατέστην:—
**A** **to be restored,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 9b25, +others; τῷ θεῷ “SIG” 459.7 (Beroea, 3rd c.BC)}}; **return** from captivity, Vett. Val.68.24; μέχρι τοῦ τὰ πράγματ᾽ ἀποκαταστῆναι till affairs **are settled,** Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 10 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἀ. εἰς τὴν ἐξ ἀρχῆς κατάστασιν **return, settle down** into.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 25.1.1, compare 2.41.14, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 34}}; of planets, **complete a revolution,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.7, +others}}; of the periodic **return** of the cosmic cycle, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.190}}; of stars, **return** to their place in the heavens, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteorologicorum librum primum commentarium - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 112.21}}; of a revolving figure, **return** to the original situation, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra}}**Con. Praef.;** of sicknesses, **subside,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 6.49}}; ἀ. εἴς τι **turn out** so and so, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.14.5}}; of sediment, **settle,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.89}}; of a spring, **recoil,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 70.46, compare 71.2.}}
**2** **to be detached from** the mainland, of an island, {{Refs|Anonymus Vat.40.}}

601:
** ἀπο-κᾰλύπτω**,
_aorist 2_ _passive_ **-καλύφην**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPR” 1.239.5 (3rd c.AD)}}, etc.:—**uncover,** τὴν κεφαλήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119; τὰ στήθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 352a}}:—in _passive_, of land **left** cultivable by the Nile {{Refs 1st c.AD+|compare (ἀποκάλυφος—, ἀρούρας β ἀποκαλυφείσης.. αἰγιαλοῦ “PIand.” 27.12, compare 27.60 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}:—_middle_, ἀποκαλύπτεσθαι τὴν κεφαλήν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 6.}}
**2** **disclose, reveal,** τόδε τῆς διανοίας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 352a; τὴντῆς ῥητορικῆς δύναμιν [prev. author] “Grg.” 455d, compare 460a}}:—_middle_, **reveal one's whole mind,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 55, 2.880e}}:—in _passive_, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.2.27, +others}}; ἀποκαλύπτεσθαι πρός τι **letone's designs** upon a thing **become known,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.62, 18.23}}:—_passive_, **to be made known,** {{NT|NT.Matt.10.26, etc.}}; of persons, {{NT|NT.2Thes.2.3, 6, 8, etc.}}; λόγοι ἀποκεκαλυμμένοι **naked,** i.e. **shameless,** words, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Vitae Homeri” 214.}}
**3** **unmask,** τινά{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 26, “Vit.Auct.” 23.}}
**II** of the epiglottis, **raise,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 422a2 (_passive_).}}

606:
** ἀπόκειμαι**,
_future_ -κείσομαι, used as _passive_ of ἀποτίθημι, **to be laid away from,** προμαθείας ἀπόκεινται ῥοαί the tides of events **lie beyond** our foresight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.46, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 110.}}
**II** _absolutely_, **to be laid up in store,** of money, ἀ. ἔνδον ἀργύριον[{{Refs 6th c.BC+|{5-6th c.BC}: Philetaerus Comicus 7.6; σῖτος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.6; παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.22}}]; τινί for one's use, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.15}}; χάρις.. ξύν᾽ ἀπόκειται (as Reisig for ξῠναπόκειται) **is laid up** as a common possession, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1752}}: hence, **to be kept in reserve,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.19, etc.}}; πολύς σοι [γέλως] ἐστὶν ἀποκείμενος you have great **store** of laughter **in reserve,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.2.15}}; ἀ. εἰς.. **to be reserved for** an occasion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 952d}}; τὸ τῆς συγγνώμης ὠφέλιμον, ἔλεος ἀ. τινί, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.42, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.31}}; σοφία ἐς ἐκείνας [τὰς τέχνας] ἀποκείσθω **let** the name of wisdom **be reserved for..,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 1}}; ἐφ᾽ ὑμῖν ἀπόκειται τὸ πεισθῆναι you **reserve** your acquiescence, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 38.5}}: with _infinitive_, ἀτυχήματα ἀπόκειταί τινι ἐνευδοκιμεῖν{{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.198; ὅσα τοῖς κακουργοῖς ἀ. παθεῖν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.8, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 51; ἀ. τοῖς ἀνθρώποις ἅπαξ ἀποθανεῖν NT.Heb.9.27; πᾶσι.. τὸ θανεῖν ἀπόκειται “epigram Gr.” 416.6 (from Alexandria).}}
**II.2** **to be buried, Not. Scav.**1923.49.
**III** **to be laid aside, neglected,** ἀ. πόρρω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 367, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.159f, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.21.}}
**III.2** ἀποκειμένη καὶ παλαιὰ φύσις **stale,** of perfume, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.46.}}
**IV** **to be exposed, lie open, to,** χώρα ἀ. βαρβάροις{{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Aedificiis” 4.2, compare 2.9.}}

608:
** ἀποκλείω**,
_future_ **-κλείσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.20}}: _Ionic dialect_**ἀποκληίω**: _Attic dialect_ **ἀποκλήω**, _future_ **-κλῃσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 775}}: _Doric dialect_ _aorist_ _imperative_ **-κλᾳξον**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.43}}:—**shut off from** or **out of,** τινὰ τῶν πυλέων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.104; δωμάτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 670}}; ἀ. τινά **shut** her **out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.43, 77; τινὰ τῇ κιλκλίδι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 775; τῇ θύρᾳ [prev. author] “Ec.” 420}}:—_middle_, ἀ. τινὰ τῆς διαβάσεως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.101:}} —_passive_, ἀ. τῆς διεξόδου [ὕδωρ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117; τῆς ὀπίσω ὁδοῦ [prev. work] 55}}; τοῦ ἄστεος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 58; ἀ. τῶν πυλῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 423 codices; τῆς θύρας 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 23; ὑπὸ τῆς ἵππου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.50.}}
**2** **shut out** or **exclude from,** τούτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.37, etc.; ἀ. τινὰ τῶν ὑπαρχόντων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.17; ἀπὸ τῶν ἀλαθῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 601; also ἀποκεκλῄκαμεν.. θεοὺς μηκέτι.. διαπερᾶν πόλιν [prev. author] “Av.” 1263:}} —_passive_, ἀ. τοῦ σίτου, τῶν προσηκόντων, **turn away from** food, **have no appetite,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 1; τῶν σιτίων [prev. author] “Vict.” 3.81, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.11; ἀ.τοῦ λόγου τυχεῖν [prev. author] 45.19; πρὸς τὰς ὁτουδήποτε ἀποκλείονται μεταδόσεις}}**refuse,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.17.}}
**II** with _accusative_ only, **shut up, close,** τὰς πύλας, τὰ ἱρά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.150, 2.133}}; τὰ.. πρὸς τὴν ἠῶ ἔχοντα τό τε Πήλιον ὄρος καὶ ἡ Ὄσσα ἀποκληΐει, of Thessaly,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|7.129; ἀ. τὰς ἐφόδους τῶν ἐπιτηδείων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.3}}:—_passive_, **to be closed,** ἀ. αἱ πύλαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117}}; ἀ. ἡ Σκυθικὴ ὑπὸ Ἀγαθύρσων, i.e. **is bounded** by them, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.100}}; of a road, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 8.4.}}
**II.2** **shut up,** as if in prison, δέμας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1388, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 719; τὴν πόλιν ἀ. μοχλοῖς [prev. author] “Lys.” 487; ἀ. τινὰ ἔνδον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.41}}:—_passive_, ἀποκλεσθαι ἐν δωματίῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.17.}}
**II.3** **shut out,** ἀ. τὴν ὄψιν **intercept,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.7}}; ἀ. τὴν βλάστην τοῦ πτεροῦ **bar** its growth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251d}}:— _passive_, τὸ φῶς ἀποκέκλεισται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 904b18.}}

609:
** ἀπο-κόπτω**,
**cut off, hew off,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, of men's limbs, κάρη ἀπέκοψε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.261; ἀπό τ᾽ αὐχένα κόψας [prev. work] 146, +others}}; in Prose, χεῖρας ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.91, etc.; ἀγκύρας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.21; γεφύρας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 26}}; **amputate,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 47.13.2; νηῶν ἀποκόψειν ἄκρα κόρυμβα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.241; ἀπὸ πείσματ᾽ ἔκοψα νεός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.127}}; ἀΐξας ἀπέκοψε παρήορον he **cut loose** the trace-horse, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.474:}} —_passive_, ἀποκεκόψονται, of buds, **will be cut off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1125, compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 11.8}}; ἀ. τὴν χεῖρα **have** it **cut off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.114}}; ἀ.τὰ γεννητικά, of eunuchs, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.89: _absolutely_, ἀποκεκομμένος}}**eunuch,** {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Deu.23.1, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Eun.” 8}}:—_middle_, **make oneself a eunuch,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Gal.5.12, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.20.19.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἀπ᾽ ἐλπίδα φημὶ κεκόφθαι ναυτιλίης νόστου τε{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1272, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.63.8; ἔλεον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.23}}; ἀ. τὸ ἀμφίβολον τῆς γνώμης **decide summarily,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.8; also ἀποκοπῆναι τῆς ἐλπίδος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 2}}; διὰ τὸ μὴ ἀποκόπτειν τὴν πολυχρόνιον ζωήν **exclude from the reckoning, despair of,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.22}}; **reject, exclude,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Sign.” 7, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.13}}:—_middle_, uncertain in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 23.}}
**3** especially of voice or breath, **cut short,** τὸν τοῦ πνεύματος τόνον{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 14, compare 22}}:—_passive_, ἀποκέκοπταί τινι ἡ φωνή{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 25, compare 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.85.}}
**4** of literary periods or phrases, **bring to an abrupt close,** δεῖ τῆ μακρᾶ -κόπτεσθαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1409a19, compare 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 18, 238.}}
**5** Grammars, in _passive_, **to be cut short by** ἀποκοπή (which see), {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 487.10, “Etymologicum Magnum” 609.54.}}
**6** **abstract** an idea or word from its context, τὸ ἀγαθὸς ἀποκοπέν{{Refs|Anonymus “in SE” 57.31.}}
**II** ἀ. τινὰ ἀπὸ τόπου **beat off from** a strong place, of soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.39, 4.2.10.}}
**III** _middle_, **smite the breast in mourning**: with _accusative_, **mourn for,** νεκρόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 628.}}

610:
** ἀπόκρῐμα**, ατος, τό,
**judicial sentence, condemnation** (={κατάκριμα}, {{NT+5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus), τὸ ἀ. τοῦ θανάτου NT.2Cor.1.9.}}
**2** (from _middle_) **answer,** δοῦναί τισι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.26b. 1}}; especially of the **answers** given by Emperors to **legationes,** ὁ ἐπὶ τῶν Ἑλληνικῶν ἀ.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“SIG” 804.5 (Cos, 1st c.AD)}}; ἐπὶ τῶν ἐπιστολῶν καὶ πρεσβειῶν καὶ ἀ.{{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see at {Διονύσιος},{{Refs 1st c.AD+|compare “IG” 12(1).2.4 (Rhodes, 1st c.AD, _plural_), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.10.6}}; also of a proconsul of Asia, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 494.18 (Milet., 2nd c.AD).}}
**2.b** **rescript,** θεοῦ Ἁδριανοῦ{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 286.1 (2nd c.AD).}}

611:
** ἀποκρίνω** [ῑ],
_future_ -κρῐνῶ, **set apart,** probably in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 5.7, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 23, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.8; χωρὶς ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302c, +others}}:—_passive_, **to be parted or separated,** ἀποκρινθέντε **parted from the throng** (of two πρόμαχοι), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.12 (nowhere else in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus); πίθηκος ᾔει θηρίων ἀποκριθείς 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 89.3}}; of the elements in cosmogony, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 9.4, 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 2, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 167; ἀπεκρίθη.. τοῦ βαρβάρου ἔθνεος τὸ Ἑλληνικόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60; χωρίς θηρίων ἡ δίαιτα ἀποκέκριται [prev. author] 2.36}}; ἀποκεκρίσθαι εἰς ἓν ὄνομα **to be separated and brought** under one name, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3}}; οὐ βεβαίως ἀπεκρίθησαν, of combatants, **separated** without decisive result, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.72.}}
**2** in Medicine texts in _passive_, **to be distinctly formed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 23}}; of the embryo, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 561a17}}; τὰ ἐν τῷ σώματι -όμενα bodily **secretions,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 14; τὰ ἐς τὴν κοιλίην ἀ. [prev. author] “Vict.” 4.89}}; but ἐς τοῦτο πάντα ἀπεκρίθη all illnesses **determined** or **ended** in this **alone,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49; also ᾗ τὰ περιττώματα ἀποκρίνεται}}**are voided,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 665b24, compare “GA” 773b35.}}
**3** **mark by a distinctive form, distinguish,** πρύμνην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.194; νόσημά τι ἀποκεκριμένον}}**specific,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 369b29.}}
**II** **choose,** ἕνα ὑμῶν ἀ. ἐξαίρετον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.130}}; ἀ. τοῦ πεζοῦ, τοῦ στρατοῦ, **choose from..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17, 25}}; δυοῖν ἀποκρίνας κακοῖν **having set apart,** i.e. **decreed,** one of two, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 640.}}
**II.2** **exclude,** πλήθει τῶν ψήφων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 946a.}}
**III** **reject on examination,** κρίνειν καὶ ἀ. {{Refs|[prev. work] 751d; ἐγκρίνειν καὶ ἀ. [prev. work] 936a}}; ἀ. τινὰ τῆς νίκης **decide** that one **has lost** the victory, **decide** it **against** one, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315b18}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 966d.}}
**IV** _middle_, ἀποκρίνομαι, _future_ -κρῐνοῦμαι, etc.: {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}} uses _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense, {{Refs|“Prt.” 358a, “Grg.” 463c}}, etc., but also in _passive_ sense (see. below):—**give answer to, reply to** question,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|uncertain 1. in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49, 8.101 (elsewhere ὑποκρ-),compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1271, “IA” 1354; ἀ. τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1245, etc.: _metaphorically_, ἀ. τοῖς πράγμασιν ὡς ἐπὶ τῶν ἐρωτημάτων 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.16.2}}; ἀ. πρός τινα, πρὸς τὸ ἐρωτώμενον, **to** a questioner or question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338d; ἀ. εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 964; ἀ. ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90: with _accusative_, ἀποκρίνεσθαι τὸ ἐρωτηθέν}} **to answer** the question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.61, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 49a, “Hp.Ma.” 287b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1007a9: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἀ. οὐδὲ γρῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 17; οὐδὲν ξυμβατικόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.71; ἀ. ἀπόκρισιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 658c:}} —_passive_, τοῦτό μοι ἀποκεκρίσθω **let** this **be** my **answer,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 187b}}; καλῶς ἄν σοι ἀπεκέκριτο your **answer would have been** sufficient, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 453d, compare “Men.” 75c, “Euthd.” 299d.}}
**IV.2** **answer charges, defend oneself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 632; ὁ ἀποκρινόμενος}}**the defendant,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.18, compare 2.4.3}}; ἀπεκρινάμην frequently in legal documents, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.31.24 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**IV.3** _aorist_ _passive_ ἀπεκρίθη, ={ἀπεκρίνατο}, he **answered,** condemned by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 86}}, is unknown in earlier _Attic dialect_, except in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.149b; but occurs in Machoap. 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.349d, “UPZ” 6.30 (2nd c.BC), “SIG” 674.61 (Narthacium, 2nd c.BC), “IG” 4.679 (Hermione, 2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.30.7, etc.; once in 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, “AJ” 9.3.1, twice in 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista, “Sol.” 5, “Demon.” 26; regular in LXX (but sometimes ἀπεκρινάμην}} in solemn language, as {{Refs|“3 Ki.” 2.1)}} and prevails in “NT” especially in the phrase ἀποκριθεὶς εἶπεν{{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Matt.3.15; ἀ. λέγει NT.Mark.8.20, +others, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.22 codices:}}_future_ ἀποκριθήσομαι in same sense, {{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Isa.14.32, +others, NT.Matt.25.45, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 4.6.}}

612:
** ἄπο-κρῐσις**, εως, ἡ,
**separation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 4; κάθαρσις ἀ. χειρόνων ἀπὸ βελτιόνων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 415d.}}
**2** in Medicine texts, **excretion** or **secretion,** γονῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Genit.” 2, σιτίων “Vict.” 4.93, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 582a4, “Pr.” 878a23, etc.}}; ἀ.σπερματική, περιττωματική, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“PA” 681b35; σπέρματος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Nat.Herc.” 908.3.}}
**3** ἀ. νοσηρή **exhalation, miasma,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος ἀνθρώπου” 9.}}
**II** (from _middle_) **decision, answer,** first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1167, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.49, 5.50 codices (ὑπόκρισις editions), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 3, “Steril.” 213, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 977; ἀ. πρὸς τὸ ἐρώτημα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.60, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.35.}}
**II.2** **defence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.65.}}
**II.3** **rescript,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Persico” 2.23}}; = **responsum,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**II.4** **embassy, commission,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Choricius Rhetor in “Rev.Phil.” 1.79.}}
**III** a kind of **dance,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

613:
** ἀποκρύπτω**,
used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _aorist 1_, _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἀποκρύπτασκε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 157:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ -εκρύβην [ῠ{{LXX|] LXX.Job.3.23:}}_future_ -κρῠβήσομαι{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Psa.18(19).6, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 10.12}}:—_middle_, _aorist 2_ **-εκρυβόμην**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.2.1}}:—**hide from, keep hidden from,** with _accusative_ et _genitive_, αἴ γάρ μιν θανάτοιο.. δυναίμην νόσφιν ἀποκρύψαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.465: with _dative_ _person_, ἀπέκρυψεν δέ μοι ἵππους 11.718}}: with _double_ _accusative_, **hide** or **keep back from** one, οῠτε σε ἀποκρύψω τὴν ἐμὴν οὐσίαν{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.28; τι ἀπό τινος LXX.4Ki.4.27}}:— _middle_, ἀποκρύπτεσθαί τινά τι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 702c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.29, etc.}}; ἀ. τι **keep** it **back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 348e, compare 327a: with _accusative_ _person_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.23, “Smp.” 1.6.}}
**2** **hide from sight, keep hidden, conceal,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.286, etc.; ἔθηκε νύκτ᾽ ἀποκρύψας φάος 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.3; τὸν ἥλιον ὑπὸ τοῦ πλήθεος τῶν ὀϊστῶν ἀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.226; ἀποκρύψει φάος νύξ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 24; χιὼν ἀ. τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.11; ἀ. τὴν σοφίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22e; ἀ. τὴν οὐσίαν ἐν ταῖς οἰκίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.42; εἰς τὸ ἄδηλον -κρύπτων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 5.7}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 424.483}}; ἀ. ἑαυτόν **efface** oneself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 393c: with}}_infinitive_, ἀποκρύπτεσθαί τι μὴ καθ᾽ ἡδονὴν ποιεῖν **to conceal one's** doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53; περὶ ὧν ἀποκρυπτόμεθα μηδένα εἰδέναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.18}}; _perfect_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense, οὐκ ἀποκέκρυπται τὴν οὐσίαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.3: _absolutely_, ἀποκρύψασθαι πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.2}}:—_passive_, τὸν Ἑλλήσποντον ὑπὸ τῶν ἀποκεκρυμμένον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.45}}; τοὺς ἀποκρυπτομένους those who **withdraw from public,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 265.}}
**3** **obscure,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 153, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1460b4, 4th c.BC: Alcidamas Rhetor “περὶ σοφιστῶν” 30, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 63.26, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.44c.}}
**II** ἀ. γῆν **lose from sight,** of ships running out to sea, opposed to ἀνοίγνυμι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.3, φεύγειν εἰς τὸ πέλαγος.. ἀποκρύψαντα γῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338a, compare 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 59.147}}; ἐπειδὴ ἀπεκρύψαμεν αὐτούς when we **got out of sight of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 2.38, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.65 (i.e. αὐτούς) τὴν θάλατταν (i.e. by marching inland) 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.473 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; ἀποκρύπτουσι Πελειάδες (i.e. ἑαυτούς) **disappear,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 179; ἄστερες ἄμφι σελάνναν ἀ. εἶδος 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 3}}; but also _passive_ of ships, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 22.5.}}

614:
** ἀπόκρῠφος**, ον,
**hidden, concealed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1070(Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν ἀποκρύφφ in **secret,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.35; ἀ. θησαυροί}}**hidden, stored up,** {{NT|NT.Col.2.3}}; **underhand,** μηδὲν ἀ. πεποιῆσθαι{{Refs|“Vita Philonidis Epicurei” p.2C.}}
**2** with _genitive_, ἀπόκρυφον πατρός **unknown to** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.11.}}
**II** **obscure, recondite, hard to understand,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 3.5.14; γράμματα 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 242}}; ἀ. σύμβολαδέλτων, of hieroglyphics, {{Refs|“Hymnus ad Isim” 10; στήλη “PMag.Par.” 1.1115}}; [βίβλος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PMag.Leid.W.” 25.14; -κρύφων μύσται 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 7.30; ἀ. αἰτία 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 1.53D., compare [same work] “in Prm.” p.549 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III** _adverb_ **-φως** **secretly,** {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Hab.3.14, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 301.5.}}

615:
** ἀποκτείνω**
(later -κτέννω (which see): **-κταίνω**{{NT|NT.2Cor.3.6, etc.)}}_future_ -κτενῶ, _Ionic dialect_ **-κτενέω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.30}}: _aorist 1_ ἀπέκτεινα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}}: _perfect_ ἀπέκτονα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.66, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 29, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.2}}; _pluperfect_ _3rd.pers.plural_ **-εκτόνεσαν**{{Refs|[prev. author] 19.148}}, _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers.singular_ **-εκτόνεε**{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.67; later ἀπεκτόνηκα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 182b19, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 24.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 16; also ἀπέκταγκα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 344, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1324b16, 18, LXX.1Ki.24.12, etc.; ἀπέκτᾰκα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.18.10}}: _aorist 2_ **-έκτᾰνον**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}}, _poetry_ _1st pers.plural_ ἀπέκταμεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.121}}, _infinitive_ -κτάμεναι, -κτάμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.165, 5.675}}:—_passive_, late (ἀποθνήσκω being used as the _passive_ by correct writers), _present_ in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 7}}: _aorist_ ἀπεκτάνθην{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 65.4, LXX.1Mac.2.9}}: _aorist 2_ _infinitive_ ἀποκτανῆναι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.284}}: _perfect_ _infinitive_ ἀπεκτάνθαι{{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.7.4, LXX.2Mac.4.36}}:—but _aorist_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense ἀπέκτατο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.437, 17.472}}; _participle_ ἀποκτάμενος{{Refs|4.494, etc.}}; compare ἀποκτείνυμι:— stronger form of κτείνω, **kill, slay,** _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, and the prevailing form in _Attic dialect_ (compare ἀποθνήσκω): once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1250, never in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, frequently in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, “Hec.” 1244, +others}}
**2** of judges, **condemn to death,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.92, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30df, etc.}}; also of the accuser, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.21, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.61}}; **put to death,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.4}}: generally of the law, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325b.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, τὸ σεμνὸν ὥς μ᾽ ἀ. τὸ σόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1064}}; σὺ μή μ᾽ ἀπόκτειν) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 1027.}}

618:
** ἀπολαμβάνω**,
_future_ -λήψομαι, in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} **-λάμψομαι**{{Refs|3.146, 9.38: _Attic dialect_}}_perfect_ ἀπείληφα, _passive_ ἀπείλημμαι, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπολέλαμμαι: in _active_ _aorist 2_ ἀπέλαβον, but in _passive_ _aorist 1_ ἀπελήφθην, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπελάμφθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}:— **take** or **receive from** another, correlated to ἀποδιδόναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 332b}}; οὐδὲν ἀ. τοῦ βίου χρηστόν (variant{ἀπολαύ-}) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.258b.}}
**2** **receive what is one's due,** μισθόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.137; ἀ. τὸν ὀφειλόμενον μισθόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.14; τὴν σὴν ξυνάορον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 654; τὰ χρήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1274; τὰ παρὰ τοῦ πατρός 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 196}}; ἀ. χρέα **have** them **paid,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.15; ὑπόσχεσιν παρά τινος ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 3.3; τὰ δίκαια 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.196: opposed to λαμβάνω, Epist. 1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.14, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.5}}; ἀ. ὅρκους **accept** them **when tendered,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.9, 18.27.}}
**3** **take of, take a part of** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 369b; ἀ. μέρος τι [prev. author] “R.” 392e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1459a35: _absolutely_ in}}_aorist_ _participle_, ἀπολαβὼν σκόπει consider it **separately,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 495e, compare “R.” 420c.}}
**4** **take away,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.43.8, 17}}; **take off,** τὸ βάρος{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 711a24.}}
**5** **hear, learn,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 614a.}}
**II** **regain, recover,** τὴν τυραννίδα, τὴν πόλιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61, 2.119, 3.146, +others; τι παρά τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.30; τὴν ἡλεμονίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.21; τὴν αὐτὴν εὐερλεσίαν 14.57: _metaphorically_, ἀ. ἑαυτόν}} **recover** oneself, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 40, +others}}
**II.2** **have rendered to one,** λόγον ἀ. **demand** an account, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.27,168.}}
**III** **take apart** or **aside,** of persons, ἀ. τινὰ μοῦνον{{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.209; αὐτὸν μόνον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 78, cf. LXX.2Mac.6.21}}; of things, μὴ μόνος τὸ χρηστὸν ἀπολαβὼν ἔχε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 451:}} —_middle_, ἀπολαβόμενος **taking** him **aside,** {{NT|NT.Mark.7.33:—}}_passive_, οἱ ἀπειλημμένοι those **set apart, recluses**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 60.10 (2nd c.BC).}}
**IV** **cut off, intercept,** λέγων ὡς ἀπολάμψοιτο συχνούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.38}}; ἀ. τείχει wall **off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.102; ἰσθμούς [prev. author] 1.7, compare 4.113}}; ἀ. εἴσω **shut up** inside, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.134}}; of contrary winds, ὅταν τύχωσιν οἱ ἄνεμοι ἀπολαβόντες αὐτούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58c; κἂν ἄνεμοι τὴν ναῦν ἀπολάβωσιν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 14}}; τὴν ἀναπνοὴν ἀ. τινός **stop** his breath, **choke** him, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 27; τὸν ἀντίπαλον ἐς πνῖγμα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.6; ἀ. τῶν σιτίων}}**spoil the appetite,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.22:}} —frequently in _passive_, ὑπ᾽ ἀνέμων ἀπολαμφθέντες **arrested** or **stopped** by contrary winds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.115, 9.114; ὑπὸ ἀπλοίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.22; νόσῳ καὶ χειμῶνι καὶ πολέμοις ἀποληφθείς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.35; ἐν ὀλίγῳ ἀπολαμφθέντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.11; ἀπολαμφθέντες πάντοθεν [prev. author] 5.101; ἐν τῇ νήσῳ [prev. author] 8.70, 76; ἐν τῆ Εὐρώπη [prev. work] 97, 108}}; ἐν τοῖς ἰδίοις λόγοις ἀ. **to be entangled** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 305d; ἐν τούτῳ τῷ κακῷ [prev. author] “Grg.” 522a; ἐς στενόν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.19.1}}; of an afflux of blood, **to be checked,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 4}}; κοιλίη, κύστις ἀπολελαμμένη, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prorrh.” 1.88, 115, etc.}}
**V** in Mathematics texts, **cut off,** ἡμικύκλιον ἀποληφθήσεται{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 375b27, compare 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “τετραγωνισμὸς παραβολῆς” 15, etc.}}; **intercept,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.Cyl.” 1.10}}; -ομένη, ἡ, **abscissa,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1.11, +others—A prose word, used by 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same places]}}

619:
** ἀπό-λαυσις**, εως, ἡ,
**act of enjoying, fruition,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.38, “OGI” 669.8 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; ἀ. ἀνεύφραντος{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 9.}}
**II** **result of enjoying, pleasure,** αἱ ἀ. αἱ σωματικαί{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1314b28, compare “EN” 1148a5, etc.}}; ὁ κατ᾽ ἀπόλαυσιν βίος a life of **pleasure,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Top.” 102b17.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_, **aduantage got from** a thing, σίτων καὶ ποτῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.33, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 11; ἀγαθῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.27}}; ἀπόλαυσιν εἰκοῦς (_accusative_ _absolutely_) **as a reward for** your resemblance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 77, compare “HF” 1370; ἀ. ἑαυτῶν ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 83a}}; ἀ. ἀδικημάτων **advantage, fruit** of them, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tyr.” 5.}}

620:
** ἀπο-λείπω**,
_aorist_ ἀπέλῐπον (ἀπέλειψα late, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 25.310d, 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 77)}}:—
**A** **leave over** or **behind,** οὐδ᾽ ἀπέλειπεν ἔγκατα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.292, compare 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 56, etc.; κλέος καὶ φήμην 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 44 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; **bequeath, Test. Epict.**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2.3, compare 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.97}}; ἀ. κληρονόμον **leave as** one's heir, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 105.3 (2nd c.AD)}}; **bequeath to posterity,** of writings, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.58, compare 7.54.}}
**A.2** **leave hold of, lose,** ψυχάν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.101 (tmesis); βίον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.” (Lyric poetry, tmesis); νέαν ἁμέραν ἀπολιπὼν θάνοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 720 (Lyric poetry): conversely, ἐμὲ μὲν ἀπολέλοιπεν ἤδη βίοτος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 185 (Lyric poetry). 3}}. **leave behind** in the race, **distance**: generally, **surpass,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.25, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.4; τινὰ περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.50}}:—more frequently in _middle_ and _passive_, see below 4. **leave undisputed**: hence, **admit,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.173, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 17, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.55, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.54; αἰτίαν νόσων ἀ. τὸ αἷμα 4th c.BC: Meno Medicus “Iatrika” 11.43; [ὁ Διοκλῆς] τὴν φρόνησιν περὶ τὴν καρδίαν ἀ. 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus}} _middle_ in {{Refs|“Rh.Mus.” 49.540.5}}. **leave, allow,** ὑπερβολὴν οὐδὲ ταῖς ἑταίραις{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.210d.}}
**A.II** **desert, abandon,** one's post, etc., οὐδ᾽ ἀπολείπουσιν κοῖλον δόμον, of bees, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.169, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.41, +others}}; ἀ. (i.e. τὴν πολιορκίην) {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.170}}; τὴν ξυμμαχίαν, τὴν ξυνωμοσίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.9, 64}}; of persons, καί σ᾽ ἀπολείψω σου λειπόμενος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1310}}; ξεῖνον πατρώϊον ἀ. **leave** him **in the lurch,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 521; ἀπολιπὼν οἴχεται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.48, compare 5.103, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 83}}; of a wife, **desert** her husband, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.14, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.4}} (not of the husband, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 9)}}; of sailors, **desert,** τὴν ναῦν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.14.2. with}}_infinitive_, ἀ. τούτους κακῶς γηράσκειν **leave** them to grow old, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.22.3}}. **leave undone** or **unsaid,** ὅσα ἀπέλιπε κτείνων τε καὶ διώκων.. σφέα ἀπετέλεσε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92. ; ὕβρεως οὐδ᾽ ὁτιοῦν ἀ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420a}}; **omit,** συχνὰ ἀπολείπω {{Refs|[prev. work] 509c.}}
**A.III** **leave open,** **leave a space,** ἀ. μεταίχμιον οὐ μέγα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.77; ἀ. ὡς πλέθρον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.11}}; μικρὸν ἀ. **leaving** a small **interval,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 27.1.}}
**A.IV** _intransitive_, **cease, fail,** τάων οὔποτε καρπὸς ἀπόλλυται οὐδ᾽ ἀπολείπει{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.117}}; opposed to γίνεται, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Diogenes Apolloniates Philosophus 7}}; of rivers, **fall, sink,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.14, 93; ἀ. τὸ ῥέεθρον [prev. author] 2.19; τῆς θαλάττης τὰ μὲν ἀπολειπούσης, τὰ δ᾽ ἐπιούσης 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 353a22}}; of swallows, δι᾽ ἔτεος ἐόντες οὐκ ἀπολείπουσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.22}}; of youth, **begin to decay,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.14}}; **fail, flag, lose heart,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.2.3}}; of the moon, **wane,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 0.98a33.}}
**A.IV.2** with _genitive_, **to be wanting of** or **in** a thing, προθυμίας οὐδὲν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.22, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 533a: frequently of numbers, μηδὲν ἀ. τῶν πέντε κτλ. [prev. author] “Lg.” 828b; τῶν εἴκοσιν ὀλίγον ἀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 573b16, etc.}}; ἀπὸ τεσσέρων πηχέων ἀ. τρεῖς δακτύλους **wanting** three fingers **of** four cubits, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, compare 7.117; μήτ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ὑπερβάλλων βοὸς ὁπλὴν μήτ᾽ ἀπολείπων 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 489: with}}_infinitive_, ὀλίγον ἀπέλιπον ἐς Ἀθήνας ἀπικέσθαι **wanted** but little of coming, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9. ; βραχὺ ἀ. διακόσιαι γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.70; οὐδὲν δ᾽ ἀπολείπετε οὕτω πολεμεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.40; ἡ πόλις μικρὸν ἀπέλιπεν ἔρημος εἶναι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tim.” I.}}
**A.IV.3** with _participle_, **leave off** doing, ἀ. λέγων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.1: _absolutely_, ὅθεν ἀπέλιπες}} from the point at which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 497c, compare “Phd.” 78b, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.12.}}
**A.IV.4** **depart from,** ἐκ τῶν Συρακουσῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.4; ἐκ τοῦ Μηδικοῦ πολέμου [prev. author] 3.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 112c.}}
**B** _middle_ (_aorist_ ἀπελιπόμην in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.399 (tmesis)), like}}_active_ {{Refs|1.1}}, **bequeath to posterity,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.134 codices}}; compare ἀπολείψεται· ἐάσεται, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**C** _passive_, **to be left behind, stay behind,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75 (variant for{ὑπο-}) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.20; μόνος ἀπολελειμμένος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.3}}]; **to be unable to follow** an argument, **be at a loss,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 192d.}}
**C.2** **to be distanced by, inferior to,** ἀ. [ἀπὸ] τῶν ἄλλων θηρίων{{Refs|Diocl.fragment 145}}; **to be inferior,** ἔν τισι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.61.}}
**C.II** **to be absent** or **distant from,** with _genitive_, πολὺ τῆς ἀληθηΐης ἀπολελειμμένοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.106, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 475d; ἥβας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 440 (Lyric poetry): with _genitive_ _person_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.40, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 192d: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 80, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 240c}}; **to be deprived of,** τοῦ σοῦ.. μὴ ἀπολείπεσθαι τάφου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1169; πατρῴας μὴ ἀ. χθονός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 35; τῶν πρὶν ἀπολειφθεὶς φρενῶν [prev. author] “Or.” 216.}}
**C.II.2** **to be wanting in, fall short of,** ὅτι τοῦ σκώπτειν ἀπελείφθη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 525}}; τοῖς ἀπολειφθεῖσι (i.e. τῆς παιδείας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.128, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.209}}; ἀπολειφθεὶς ἠμῶν **without** our **cognizance,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.36; ἀπολειφθῆναι τῶν πραγμάτων}}**to be left in ignorance of..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 27.2}}; καιροῦ ἀ. **miss** the opportunity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 34.38, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.19}}; θεάματος, ἑορτῆς ἀ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 15.1, “Sacr.” 1; εἰσβολῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.31.}}
**C.II.3** **remain to be done,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.39.12: _impersonal_, ἀπολείπεται λέγειν, διδάσκειν, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.85, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.1.}}

622:
Included with: ** ἀπόλλῡμι**
or **ἀπολλύω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 608e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1297a12, but _falsa lectio_ in 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 580}}; the form is rejected by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.10 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 12)}}, _imperfect_ ἀπώλλυν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 652(Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1360, ἀπώλλυον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.58:}}_future_ ἀπολέσω, _Epic dialect_ ἀπολέσσω, _Attic dialect_ ἀπολῶ, _Ionic dialect_ ἀπολέω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, +others:}}_aorist_ ἀπώλεσα, _Epic dialect_ ἀπόλεσσα: _perfect_ ἀπολώλεκα:—frequently in tmesi in _Epic dialect_; _preposition_ postponed in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.534:}} —stronger form of ὄλλυμι, **destroy utterly, kill,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly of death in battle, ἀπώλεσε λαὸν Ἀχαιῶν{{Refs|11.5.758, +others; ἐκπάγλως ἀπόλεσσαν [prev. work] 1.268}}; also of things, **demolish, lay waste,** ἀπώλεσεν Ἴλιον ἱρήν {{Refs|[prev. work] 5.648}}, etc.; generally, βίοτον δ᾽ ἀπὸ πάμπαν ὀλέσσει **will waste** my substance, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.49}}; οἵ μ᾽ ἀπωλλύτην **sought to destroy** me (_imperfect_ sense), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1454}}; in pregnant sense, ἐπεί με γᾶς ἐκ πατρίας ἀπώλεσε **drove** me **ruined** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 946}}; τῆς παρ᾽ ἡμέραν χάριτος τὰ μέγιστα τῆς πόλεως ἀ. for the sake of.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.70.}}
**2** λόγοις or λέγων ἀ. τινά **talk** or **bore** one **to death,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1360, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 892 (Lyric poetry)}}: hence, alone, in _future_ ἀπολεῖς με{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 470; οἴμ᾽ ὡς ἀπολεῖς με 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.20}}; ἀπολεῖ μ᾽ οὑτοσί by his questions, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 222.8, etc.}}
**3** **ruin** a woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.8.}}
**II** **lose,** πατέρ᾽ ἐσθλὸν ἀπώλεσα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.82, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 272; ἀπώλεσε νόστιμον ἦμαρ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.354}}; ἀπὸ θυμὸν ὀλέσσαι **lose** one's life,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.16.861, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.350}}; θυμὸν οὐκ ἀπώλεσεν **loses** not his spirit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 26; ἔλεον ἀπώλεσεν 11.24.44}}; frequently of things, ἡ τοῦ πλέονος ἐπιθυμίη τὸ παρεὸν ἀπόλλυσι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 224; ἵππους ἑβδομήκοντα ἀπολλύασι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.51; ἀπώλεσαν τὴν ἀρχὴν ὑπὸ Περσῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.11, compare 7.2.22; μηδὲν ἀπολλὺς τοῦ ὄγκου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155c}}; ἀ. οὐσίαν, ={ἀπόλλυσθαι}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prm.” 163d.}}
**B** _middle_, ἀπόλλῠμαι: _future_ -ολοῦμαι, _Ionic dialect_ **-ολέομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.218:}}_aorist 2_ -ωλόμην: _perfect_ -όλωλα, whence the barbarous _imperfect_ ἀπόλωλο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1212:}}_pluperfect_ in _Attic dialect_ Prose sometimes written ἀπωλώλειν in codices, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.133, 7.27}}:—**perish, die,** {{Refs|11.1.117}}, etc.; **cease to exist,** opposed to γίγνεσθαι, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 156b, etc.: sometimes with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἀπόλωλε κακὸν μόρον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.166; ἀπωλόμεθ᾽ αἰπὺν ὄλεθρον [prev. work] 9.303: with _dative_ modi, ἀπώλετο λυγρῷ ὀλέθρῳ (variant{λυγρὸν ὄλεθρον}) [prev. work] 3.87; ἀ. ὑπό τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.126}}]; simply, **to be undone,** αὐτῶν.. ἀπωλόμεθ᾽ ἀφραδίῃσιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.27; ἀπωλώλει τῷ φόβῳ μή.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.2: frequently in _Attic dialect_, especially in}}_perfect_, ἀπόλωλας you **are lost,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1077; ἀπωλόμεθ᾽ ἂν εἰ μὴ ἀπολώλειμεν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.185f; ἱκανὸν χρόνον ἀπολλύμεθα καὶ κατατετρίμμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 355; βλέπειν ἀπολωλός 3rd c.AD: Philostratus Junior Sophista “Imagines” 2:—as an imprecation, κάκιστ᾽ ἀπολοίμην εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 151, +others; κακὸς κακῶς ἀπόλοιθ᾽ ὅστις.. 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 116; ἐξώλης ἀπόλοιθ᾽ ὅστις.. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 154}}; ἀπολλύμενος, opposed to σῳζόμενος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.36, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.469d: frequently in}}_participle_ _future_, κάκιστ᾽ ἀπολούμενε o **destined to a** miserable **end!** i.e. o **thou villain, scoundrel, knave!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 713, compare 456, “Ach.” 865, “Pax” 2; ὁ κάκιστ᾽ ἀνέμων ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 14.2.}}
**B.2** in **NT, perish,** in theol. sense, {{NT|NT.John.3.16, +others}}; οἱ ἀπολλύμενοι, opposed to οἱ σῳζόμενοι, {{NT|NT.1Cor.1.18.}}
**B.II** **to be lost,** ὕδωρ ἀπολέσκετ᾽ (of the water eluding Tantalus) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.586}}; οὔποτε καρπὸς ἀπόλλυται never **falls untimely,**{{LXX+8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.117; ἀπό τέ σφισιν ὕπνος ὄλωλεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.186; γέλως ἐξ ἀνθρώπων ἀπόλωλεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.15; ἀπολόμενον ἀργύριον 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 54; ἀπώλοντο οἱ ὄνοι LXX.1Ki.9.3.}}

626:
** ἀπολογ-έομαι**,
_aorist_ ἀπελογησάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 41, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.13, but _falsa lectio_ in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 261c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.3}}; also _aorist_ _passive_ ἀπελογήθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.1, +others, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 12 (probably suprious in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.4.13)}}: _perfect_ ἀπολελόγημαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.33, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.218 (in _passive_ sense, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 607b)}}:—**speak in defence, defend oneself,** opposed to κατηγορεῖν, περί τινος **about** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.7, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.72}}; πρὸς τὴν μαρτυρίαν **in reference** or **answer to** the evidence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.3, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.29; πρός τινας}}**before..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 357, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.6.4: later, with _dative_, κατηγορίαις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 23}}; ἀ. ὑπέρ τινος **speak in** another's **behalf,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.161, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 41, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 488a, etc.}}; ἀ. ὑπέρ τινος **speak in support of** a fact, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.1; ὑπὲρ τῆς ἀδικίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480b}}; πρὸς Μέλητον **in answer to** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ap.” 24b: _absolutely_, παρὼν ἀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.136, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 188; ὁ ἀπολογούμενος}}**the defendant,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” 778, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.6.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **defend oneself against,** ἀ. τὰς διαβολάς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.109}}; τὰς πράξεις **defend** what one has done, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.92.}}
**3** ἀ. τι ἔς τι **allege in one's defence** against a charge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.63; ἀ. πρὸς τὰ κατηγορημένα μηδέν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.38; τί ποτε ἀπολογήσεσθαι μέλλει μο; 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.7 codices; ταῦτα ἀ. ὡς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69d; ἔργοις καλλίστοις ἀ. ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.65; ἀ. ὅτι οὐδένα ἀδικῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.22; ἀ. ἀπολογίαν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hes.” 6.}}
**4** ἀ. δίκην θανάτου **speak against** sentence of death **passing on one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68.}} —Prose word, used once in Trag., see above—The _preposition_ ἀπό implies the **remoual** of a charge.

628:
** ἀπο-λούω**,
**I** with _accusative_ _of things_, **wash off,** λούειν ἄπο βρότον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.7:}} —_middle_, ὄφρ᾽.. ἅλμην ὤμοιϊν ἀπολούσομαι that I **may wash** the brine **from off my** shoulders, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.219}}; of baptism, ἀ. τὰς ἁμαρτίας{{NT|NT.Act.22.16.}}
**I.2** with _accusative_ _person_, **wash clean,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 118 (ἀπέλου for ἀπέλοε, see at {λούω}), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 405b, 406a}}]:—_middle_, **wash away from oneself,** λούσασθαι ἄπο βρότον αἱματόεντα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.41; τὸ σῶμα ἀπελούετο 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 1.13}}: in archaic style, ἀπολούμενος{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Legal.” 2, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.97e, 98a.}}
**I.3** with _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, ὄφρα τάχιστα Πάτροκλον λούσειαν ἄπο βρότον **might wash** the gore **off** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.345: with _genitive_ _of things_, καί μ᾽ ἀπέλουσε λύθρου “epigram Gr.” 314.6 (from Smyrna).}}
**I.4** ἀπολουσέμεναι· κολ[λ]οβώσειν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|(5th c.AD: Cypria), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; compare ἀπολέπω.

629:
** ἀπολύτρ-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**ransoming,** αἰχμαλώτων{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 24 (_plural_), compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.2.3, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.463.}}
**II** **redemption by payment of ransom, deliverance, **{{NT|NT.Luke.21.28, NT.Rom.3.24, +others}}; of Nebuchadnezzar's **recovery,** {{LXX|LXX.Dan.4.30c}}; in **NT, redemption,** {{NT|NT.Rom.3.24, +others}}

630:
** ἀπολύω**
[see. λύω], _future_ -λύσω, etc.: _future_ _passive_
**A** ἀπολελύσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.37}}:—**loose from,** ἱμάντα θοῶς ἀπέλυσε κορώνης{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.46}}; ὄφρ᾽ ἀπὸ τοίχους λῦσε κλύδων τρόπιος the sides of the ship **from** the keel, {{Refs|[prev. work] 12.420}}; **undo,** ἀπὸ κρήδεμνον ἔλυσεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.392; ἐπιδέσματα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 25.}}
**A.2** **set free, release, relieve from,** ἀ. τινὰ τῆς φρουρῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30; τῆς ἐπιμελείας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.47; τῶν ἐκεῖ κακῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 365a; τὴν ψυχὴν ἀπὸ τῆς τοῦ σώματος κοινωνίας [prev. author] “Phd.” 65a, compare 67a}}; ἀ. τῆς μετρήσεως **save** them **from the trouble** of measuring, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1257a40}}:— _passive_, **to be set free,** τῶν δεινῶν, φόβου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70, 7.56, etc.}}
**A.2.b** frequently in legal sense, ἀ. τῆς αἰτίης **acquit of** the charge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.15}}; opposed to καταψηφίζω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 262; τῆς εὐθύνης 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 571: with}}_infinitive_, ἀ. τινὰ μὴ φῶρα εἶναι **acquit of** being a thief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.174; so ἀπολύεται μὴ ἀδικεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.95, compare 128}}: _absolutely_, **acquit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 988, 1000, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.20, etc.}}
**A.II** in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” always, ={ἀπολυτρόω}}}], **release on receipt of ransom,** οὐδ᾽ ἀπέλυσε θύγατρα καὶ οὐκ ἀπεδέξατ᾽ ἄποινα{{Refs|1.95; Ἕκτορ᾽ ἔχει.. οὐδ᾽ ἀπέλυσεν 24.115, +others}}:—_middle_, **set free by payment of ransom, ransom, redeem,** χαλκοῦ τε χρυσοῦ τ᾽ ἀπολυσόμεθ᾽ **at a price of..,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.50}} (but _active_ in Prose, ἀπολύειν πολλῶν χρημάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.21).}}
**A.II.2** **let go, let alone, leave** one, of an illness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 564.}}
**A.III** **discharge, disband** an army, ἀ. οἴκαδε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.21}}; generally, **dismiss, discharge,** ἐμὲ.. ἀπέλυσ᾽ ἄδειπνον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1155, compare 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.96.}}
**A.III.2** **divorce** a wife, {{NT+1st c.BC+|NT.Matt.1.19, etc.; τὸν ἄνδρα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.18.}}
**A.III.3** **do away with, remove,** αἰσχύνην{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.47}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.5.}}
**A.III.4** **discharge** or **pay** a debt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 417b}}; **pay,** ἀ. τὸν χαλκόν{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PTeb.” 490 (1st c.BC)}}; **pay off** a mortgage, {{Refs|“POxy.” 509.15.}}
**A.III.5** **dismiss** a charge, εἰσαγγελία ὑπὸ τοῦ κατηγόρου ἀπολελυμένη{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 38.}}
**A.IV** ἀ. ἀνδράποδα Θρᾳξίν **sell,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.20}}; ἀ. οἰκίαν τινί **sell** a mortgaged house **outright,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.33.}}
**A.V** **deliver,** τί τινι{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 123.2 (3rd c.AD):}} —_passive_ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 228.6 (3rd c.AD).}}
**A.VI** **begin to count,** [μοίρας] ἀπό.. Vett. Val. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|135: _absolutely_, [prev. author] 19.19, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “Q.” 2.}}
**A.VII** _intransitive_, **depart** (cf. B.IV, C. 2), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.69.14, +others}}
**B** _middle_ with _aorist 2_ ἀπελύμην (in _passive_ sense), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.128}}:— **redeem,** see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.}}
**B.II** ἀπολύεσθαι διαβολάς **do away with, refute** calumnies **against one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.87, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 37b, +others: _absolutely_, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1416b9.}}
**B.II.2** τὴν αἰτίαν, τὰς βλασφημίας, τὰ κατηγορημένα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.75, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.2, 18.4: with _genitive_, τῶν εἰς Ἀριστόβουλον -σασθαι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.3.5.}}
**B.II.2.b** **refute,** τοὺς ἐναντίους λόγους{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 126 ter: _absolutely_, ὁ δὲ ἀπολυόμενος ἔφη}}**in defence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.59.}}
**B.III** like _active_, **acquit,** τοῦ μὴ κακῶς ἔχειν ἀλλ᾽ ὀρθῶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 637c.}}
**B.III.2** **release from,** τοὺς Ἕλληνας ἀ. δουλείας{{Refs|[prev. author] “Mx.” 245a.}}
**B.IV** like _passive_ (with {{Refs|11)}}**depart,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1314}}; also, **put off,** πνεῦμα ἀ.{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.276 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus); but πνεῦμα μελῶν ἀπέλυε “IG” 14.607e (from Carales).}}
**C** _passive_, **to be released,** ἐλπίζων τοὺς υἱέας τῆς στρατηΐης ἀπολελύσθαι **from** military service, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.84, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.37}}; τῆς ἀρχῆς ἀπολυθῆναι βουλόμενοι **to be freed from** their rule, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8; τῶν δεινῶν μηδέποτε οἴεσθαι ἀπολυθήσεσθαι [prev. author] 1.70; τῆς ὑποψίας 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.3; τῆς μιαρίας [prev. work] 3.11}}: _absolutely_, **to be acquitted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.29}}; **to be absolved from,** τῶν ἀδικημάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113d.}}
**C.II** of combatants, **to be separated, part,** οὐ ῥᾳδίως ἀπελύοντο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.49}}; generally, **to be separated** or **detached,** ἀλλήλων or ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1031b3, “Ph.” 185a28; ἀ. τὰ ᾠὰ τῆς ὑστέρας [prev. author] “GA” 754b18, +others}}; ἀπολελυμένος, _absolutely_, **detached,** αἰδοῖον, γλῶττα, ὄρχεις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 500b2, 533a27, 535b2}}; τὴν γλῶτταν ἀ. **having** its tongue **detached,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 319, +others}}; also, **distinct, differentiated,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 497b22.}}
**C.II.2** **depart,** ἔθανες, ἀπελύθης, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1268 (Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.58.4, +others, LXX.Num.20.29, +others; compare above 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus IV.}}
**C.III** of a child, **to be brought forth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 11, compare 24, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 745b11}}; of the mother, **to be delivered,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.2.17.}}
**C.IV** **to be annulled,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1156a22.}}
**C.V** ἀπολελυμένος, η, ον, **absolute,** especially in Grammars, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 636.15, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 97.20, +others}}: also, **general,** of meaning, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Olympiodorus Alchemista p.72B.}}
**C.VI** of metres, **irregular, without strophic responsion,** {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus “Poëm.” 5.}}

631:
** ἀπομάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἀπομάττω**, **wipe off,** δάκρυα χλαμύδι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.26.3, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 21}}:—_middle_, **wipe off oneself,** ὕδατος ἄχνην{{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 14; τὸν κονιορτόν τισι NT.Luke.10.11}}; **wipe,** τι {{Refs|“POxy.” 1381.133}}: _absolutely_, **dry oneself,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.203b.}}
**2** **wipe clean,** especially in magical ceremonies, ἀπομάττων [αὐτοὺς] τῷ πηλῷ καὶ τοῖς πιτύροις{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.259, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 7, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus s. vv. μαγίδες, μαγμόν}}: —_middle_, **wipe for oneself,** Ἀχιλλείων ἀπομάττει you **wipe your hands on** the finest bread, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 819; χεῖρας χειρομάκτρῳ ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.410b}}: _absolutely_, **wipe one's mouth,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus 30}}; of a serpent, ἀ. τὸν ἰόν **get rid of** its poison, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 372.}}
**II** **wipe off** or **level** corn in a measure **with a strickle**: hence χοίνικα ἀ. **give scant** measure, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 25}}; κενεὰν ἀπομάξαι (i.e. χοίνικα) **level** an empty measure, i.e. labour in vain, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.95.}}
**III** **take an impression of,** ἔν τισι τῶν μαλακῶν σχήματα ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 50e}}:—_middle_, **model,** as a sculptor, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.19, “APl.” 4.120 (1st c.BC: Archias or 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus)}}: _metaphorically_, **take impressions,** ὅθεν ἡμὴ φρὴν ἀπομαξαμένη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1040; τὰς ἰδέας 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).655}}; ἀ. παρ᾽ ἀλλήλων one from another, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1172a12}}: generally, **copy, imitate,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Veterum Censura” 3.2; ἤθεα ἀ. τεκούσης 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 46.18, compare 48.229}}: with _genitive_, **model oneself upon,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 27.}}

632:
** ἀπονέμ-ω**,
_future_ **-νεμῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 65b}}:—**portion out, impart, assign,** ἡμῖν.. ταῦτ᾽ ἀπένειμε τύχη{{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 100; βωμοὺς καὶ ἀγάλματα θεοῖσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.4; τὸ πρέπον ἑκατέροις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 757c; τῷ θεῷ τοῦτο γέρας [prev. author] “Prt.” 341e; τοῖς θεοῖς χάριτας “SIG” 708.33, cf. NT.1Pet.3.7; τὸ καλῶς ἀποθανεῖν ἴδιον τοῖς σπουδαίοις ἡ φύσις ἀπένειμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.43}}; _aorist_ _imperative_ ἀπόνειμον **render, impart,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.47; τῇ συγγνώμῃ πλέον ἢ τῷ δικαίῳ ἀπονέμειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.55}}:—_middle_, **assign** or **take to oneself,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 267a, “Lg.” 739b; ἀπονέμεσθαί τι}}**feed on,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1289}}; ἀπονέμεσθαι τῶν πατρῴων **help oneself to a share** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574a}}:—_passive_, **to be assigned,** τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1123b35}}; **to be rendered,** θεῷ{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 11.}}
**II** **part off, divide,** of logical division, ἐπὶ τἀναντία ἀ. τοῖς ὀνόμασι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 307b:}} — in _passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 276d, 280d.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be taken away, subtracted,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 771c, 848a.}}

633:
** ἀπονίζω**,
later **ἀπονευρ-νίπτω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|D.S 4.59, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 18, and once as variant in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, see below:— **wash off,** ἀπονίψαντες.. βρότον ἐξ ὠτειλῶν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.18), compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.425 (tmesis)}}:—_middle_, **wash off from oneself,** ἱδρῶ πολλὸν ἀπονίζοντο θαλάσσῃ {{Refs|[prev. work] 10.572.}}
**II** **wash clean,** especially of the hands and feet, τὴν ἀπονίζουσα φρασάμην I perceived it (the scar) **as** I **was washing his feet,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.75; ὅταν ἡ θυγάτηρ μ᾽ ἀπονίζῃ καὶ τὼ πόδ᾽ ἀλείφῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 608, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 60; ἓ μὲν ἔφη ἀ. τὸν παῖδα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175a:}} —_middle_, χρῶτ᾽ ἀπονίπτεσθαι **wash one's** body, variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.179, compare 172; χεῖράς τε πόδας τε [prev. work] 22.478: _absolutely_, οἷον εἴ τις εἰς πηλὸν ἐμβὰς πηλῷ -νίζοιτο 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 5}}; **wash one's hands** (especially after meals,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristophanes Byzantinus Philosophus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.408f), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.89; ἐγὼ μὲν ἀποτρέχων ἀπονίψομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1163; ἀπονίψασθαι δοτέον}}**water to wash with,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 250, compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 136}}; so in _perfect_ _passive_, ἀπονενίμμεθ᾽{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1217; ἀπονενιμμένος [prev. author] “Ec.” 419}}; also in late Prose, see above; τῆς κρήνης -νιψάμενος{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.1; but ἀπονίψασθαι τὸ πρόσωπον ἀπὸ τᾶς κράνας “IG” 4.951.63 (Epid.).}}
**II.2** rarely of things, ἀ. τὴν κύλικα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 41.}}

634:
** ἀποπίπτω**,
**fall off from,** ἐκ πέτρης{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.7; ἀπὸ τῶν φιαλέων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130}}; τοῦ κολεοῦ ἀ. ὁ μύκης{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 64, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 22 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; ἀ. τῶν ἵππων}}**slip off,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.21.3.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **fall off,** στιλπναὶ δ᾽ ἀπέπιπτον ἔερσαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.351, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 557b29.}}
**II** **miss** or **fail in obtaining,** τῆς ἐλπίδος ἀ.{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.7.1; τἀγαθοῦ 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 13}}; **fail to record, let slip,** τῶν ἀναγκαιοτέρων{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.84}}: _absolutely_, **to be disappointed, fail,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.36.5, “UPZ” 70.27(2nd c.BC).}}

635:
** ἀποπλᾰν-άω**,
_future_ -ήσω, **lead astray, make to digress,** λόγον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 34, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 21; ἀ. τινὰ ἀπὸ τῆς ὑποθέσεως 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.176}}:— _passive_,**wander away from,** τῆς ὑποθέσεως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.77}}: _absolutely_, of leaderless wasps, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 554b23}}; **wander from the truth,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Metaphysica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 139.12, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.33.}}
**II** **distribute,** in _passive_, ἀποπλανᾶται ἐς πάντα αἷμα καὶ πνεῦμα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τροφῆς” 33.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **seduce, beguile,** τοὺς ἐκλεκτούς{{NT|NT.Mark.13.22.}}

637:
** ἀπο-πλύνω** [ῡ],
_future_ -ῠνῶ, **wash well, wash away,** λάϊγγας.. ἀποπλύνεσκε θάλασσα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.95; τὸ περὶ τὴν γλῶτταν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 65d; τὰς χεῖρας 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.409c; τινὰ βαφῆς ἀτόπου 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.22.}}
**2** **wash away,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 443b7 (_passive_).}}

638:
** ἀποπνίγω** [ῑ],
_future_ **-πνίξω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Comedy texts 198, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 171}}: _aorist_ _infinitive_ ἀποπνεῖξαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“GDI” 2171.18 (Delph., 1st c.BC)}}:—**choke, throttle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.169, +others; τὰς ἀναπνοάς 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 9; τοὺς πατέρας τ᾽ ἦγχον.. καὶ τοὺς πάππους ἀπέπνιγον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1039}}; **suffocate,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.” 893}}; of plants, **choke, **{{NT|NT.Matt.13.7, NT.Luke.8.7}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-πνῐγήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1504}} also **-πεπνίξομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.463 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}: _aorist_ I ἀπεπνίχθην{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.7:}}_aorist 2_ ἀπεπνίγην [ῐ] (see. below): _perfect_ _participle_ **-πεπνιγμένος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72}}:—**to bechoked, suffocated,** τρώγων ἐρεβίνθους ἀπεπνίγη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 159, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 266}}; **to be drowned,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 172, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.6, 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 352.}}
**b** generally, **cut off, kill,** λιμῷ τινα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.9}}:—_passive_, πόλις ἀ. τῇ τῶν ἀναγκαίων σπάνει{{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 26.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **choke one with vexation,** ἀποπνίξεις με λαλῶν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus [same place]; ἦ γάρ [με] γειτονεῦσ᾽ ἀποπνίγεις 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.300}}:—_passive_, **to be choked with rage,** ἐπί τινι **at** a thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.199, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 16.7; πρός τι 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 63.15.}}

639:
** ἀπορ-έω**,
**B** _Laconian dialect_ _1st pers.plural_ ἀπορίομες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.23}}: _aorist_ ἠπόρησα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.63, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἠπόρηκα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 244a, etc.:}} —_passive_, _future_ ἀπορηθήσομαι (συν-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.5}}, but _middle_ in _passive_ sense ἀπορήσομαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1200a11:}}_aorist_ ἠπορήθην, _perfect_ ἠπόρημαι, both in _active_ and _passive_ sense (see. below):—**to be** ἄπορος, i. e. **without means** or **resource**: hence,
**B.1** **to be at a loss, be in doubt, be puzzled,** mostly followed by _relative_ clause, ἀ. ὅκως διαβήσεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75; ὅτῳ τρόπῳ διασωθήσεται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.109; ὅ τι λέξω δ᾽ ἀπορῶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 486(Lyric poetry)}}; ἀ. ὅπῃ, ὁπόθεν, ὅποι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107, 8.80, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.44; ὅτι χρὴ ποιεῖν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.5.38; τίνα χρὴ τρόπον.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.3; ἀ. εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326e; πότερα.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.6; ἀ. ὁποτέραν τῶν ὁδῶν τράπηται [prev. work] 2.1.21}}; ἀ. μή.. **fear** lest.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.142d: with _accusative_ added, ἀ. τὴν ἔλασιν ὅκως διεκπερᾷ}} **to be at a loss about** his march, how to cross, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.4: with _accusative_ only, ἀ. τὴν ἐξαγωγήν}} **to be at a loss about** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.179, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 664, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 348c, +others}}: also with _infinitive_, **to be at a loss how** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 590, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 262e, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.7:—also ἀ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, “Grg.” 462b, +others; ἀ. διὰ τὸ πλῆθος τῶν ἁμαρτημάτων ὅθεν ἄρξομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.10; ἐς πολλά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1243: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.134}}; οὐκ ἀπορήσας without **hesitation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.159; τὸ δ᾽ ἀπορεῖν ἀνδρὸς κακοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 106, etc.}}:— _middle_ used like _active_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. ; ὡς ἠπόρημαι.. τάδε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 537; ἠπορούμην ὅτι χρησαίμην 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321c}}: so in _aorist_ _passive_, πολλὰ.. ἀπορηθείς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.53.}}
**B.2** in Dialectic, **start a question, raise a difficulty,** ἀπορία ἣν ἀπορεῖς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 324d; ἀ. περί τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 194a15, +others; τὰ αὐτὰ περί τινος “Metaphorical” 1085a35; ἀ. πότερον.. “Pol.” 1283b36; ἀπορήσειε δ᾽ ἄν τις τί.. “EN” 1096a34, compare 1145b21, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.64.1, +others:}} —_passive_, τὸ ἀπορούμενον, τὸ ἀπορηθέν, **the difficulty just started, the puzzle before us,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 243b, “Lg.” 799c, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 1; τὰ ἠπορημένα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281a38}}; ἀπορεῖται **there is a question** or **difficulty,** πότερον..{{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1c99 b9; μή.. [prev. work] 1159a6.}}
**B.3** _passive_, of things, **to be left wanting, left unprovided for,** τῶν δεομένων γίγνεσθαι οὐδὲν ἀπορεῖται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 13.7, compare “Oec.” 8.10}}; **to fail, turn out a failure,** opposed to εὐπορεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 47.}}
**B.II** with _genitive_ _of things_, **to be at a loss for, in want of,** ἀπορεῖς δὲ τοῦ σ;{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 898; ἀλφίτων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 636; πάντων [prev. author] “Pl.” 531}}; τροφῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Thuc. 8.81; ξυμμάχων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.39; τοσαύτης δαπάνης [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.3.5; λόγων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193e}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 925b.}}
**B.III** ἀ. τινί **to be at a loss** by reason of, by means of something, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.147.}}
**B.IV** **to be in want, be poor,** opposed to εὐπορέω, in _middle_, ὅταν ἀπορῆταί τις{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 123}}, but _active_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 11, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 953.19}}; opposed to πλουτέω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203e}}:—_passive_, ἄνθρωπος ἠπορημένος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 249.—Chiefly Prose and Comedy texts; never in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, thrice in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, twice in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}}

640:
** ἀπορία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, (ἄπορος) **being** ἄπορος: hence,
**I** of places, **difficulty of passing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.10.}}
**II** of things, **difficulty, straits,** in _singular_ and _plural_, ἐς ἀπορίην πολλὴν ἀπιγμένος, ἀπειλημένος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79, 2.141}}; ἐν ἀπορίῃ or ἐν ἀποίῃσι ἔχεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.98, 4.131, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.66; ἀπορίῃσιν ἐνείχετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.190; ἀπορίην ἐρωτηθέντι παρασχεῖν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 13, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.1}}; ἀπορια τελέθει, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.105, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 788c; εἰς φρέατα καὶ πᾶσαν ἀ. ἐμπίπτων [prev. author] “Tht.” 174c: with _genitive_ _of things_, ἀ. τοῦ μὴ γινώσκειν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 1}}; ἀ. τοῦ μὴ ἡσυχάζειν **impossibility** of keeping quiet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49; ἀ. τῆς προσορμίσεως [prev. author] 4.10; ἀ. τοῦ ἀνακαθαίρεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 678d.}}
**II.2** **not providing** a thing, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Men.” 78e.}}
**III** of persons, **difficulty of dealing with** or **getting at,** τῶν Σκυθέων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.83; τοῦ ἀποκτείναντος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.2.}}
**III.2** **being at a loss, embarrassment, perplexity,** ἀ. τοῦ δυστυχεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 971, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44, 75, etc.; ἀ. ἐν τῷ λόγῷ συμβᾶσα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.41}}; **distress, discomfort,** in illness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.42, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.5: hence _metaphorically_, ὠδίνουσι καὶ ἀπορίας ἐμπίμπλανται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 151a.}}
**III.3** ἀ. τινός **lack of** a person or thing, σοφῶν ἀνδρῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 806}}; τροφῆς, χρημάτων, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.11, 7.48}}; ἀπώλλυντο.. ἀπορίᾳ τοῦ θεραπεύοντος **for want of one** to attend to them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.51; ἀ. λόγων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38d}}; ἀ. πλοίων **shortage** of ships, {{Refs|“CPHerm.” 6.10}}: _absolutely_, **need, poverty,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.123; ἀ. καὶ πενία 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.144}}; opposed to εὐπορία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1279b27: in _plural_, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.146.}}
**IV** in Dialectic, **question for discussion, difficulty, puzzle,** ἀπορίᾳ σχόμενος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321c}}; ἀ. ἣν ἀπορεῖς{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 324d; ἡ ἀ. ἰσότης ἐναντίων λογισμῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 145b1, +others; ἔχει ἀπορίαν περί τινος [prev. author] “Pol.” 1285b28; αἱ μὲν οὖν ἀ. τοιαῦταί τινες συμβαίνουσιν [prev. author] “EN” 1146b6; οὐδεμίαν ποιήσει ἀ. [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1085a27}}; ἀ. λύειν, διαλύειν, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “MM” 1201b1, “Metaphorical” 1062b31; ἀπορίᾳ ἀπορίαν λύειν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.37.}}

641:
** ἀπο-ρρίπτω**,
_poetry_ ἀπορίπτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.37, later ἀπορριπτέω 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.42, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 39, “Cat.Ma.” 5, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 12, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.9.2, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 74.1}}, _future_ **-ρρίψω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 215}}: _perfect_ ἀπέρριφα{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.40.15}}:—**throw away, put away,** μῆνιν, μηνιθμόν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.517, 16.282; ἀπὸ κρόκεον ῥίψαις.. εἷμα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.232}}; ἀπορριψοντι ἐοικώς like one **about to cast** [a net], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 215; ἀ. ἀπὸ τοῦ στόματος}}**spit,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 19.4}}; **vomit,** τὴν τροφήν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Asclepiades Junior Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.162}}; **cast up,** of a river, τοὺς νεκροὺς τῶν ῥευμάτων{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.60c.}}
**II** **cast forth from** one's country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 914; ἀπωστὸς γῆς ἀπορριφθήσομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1019; ἀπερριμμένοι}}**outcasts,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.48, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.10}}; of things, **reject,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PBaden 19.12 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὰ φαῦλα καὶ ἀπερρ. τῶν ἐδεσμάτων{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.12.2.}}
**II.2** **disown reject,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.38, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1018.}}
**II.3** **throw aside, set at naught,** ἡ ἡμετέρη εὐδαιμονίη οὕτω τοι ἀπέρριπται ἐς τὸ μηδέν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; Κύπρις δ᾽ ἄτιμος τᾠδ᾽ ἀ. λόγῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 215; ὅταν.. τὰ χρηστὰ ἀπορρίπτηται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.75.}}
**III** of words, **utter,** especially in disparagement, ἔς τινα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153, 4.142 (_passive_), 8.92: generally, ἀ. ἔπος}} **let fall** a word, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.69; χαμαιπετὲς ἔπος ἀ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.37; λόγον ἀχρεῖον 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 11.3; μηδ᾽ ἀπορριφθῇ λόγος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 484.}}
**IV** _intransitive_, **throw oneself down, leap off,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Acts.27.43, 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 3.5.}}

643:
** ἀποσκευάξω**,
**pull off,** τὴν ὀροφήν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 128}}; **clear away** tables, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; **strip of furniture,** οἰκίαν{{LXX|LXX.Lev.14.36.}}
**2** ={ξημιόω}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** mostly in _middle_, **pack up and carry off,** ἀ. τι τῶν ἰδίων{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 633.68 (Milet., 2nd c.BC), compare588.50 ([prev. work], 2nd c.BC, _passive_), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.26.6, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.23}}: _absolutely_, **pack up and depart,** {{NT|NT.Acts.21.15.}}
**II.2** **get rid of,** τὰ ἐνοχλοῦντα (={ἀποπατέω},{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.91), 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.13.4}}; **remove,** τῆς φιλίας τινά{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cal.” 12}}; **make away with, kill,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Tyr.” 1, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.5.1}}; **get rid of, confute,** τοὺς σοφιστάς{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.19}}:—_passive_, **to be expelled,** τῶν σωμάτων{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Ther.Praef.”}}
**II.3** **repel,** αἰτίαν, διαβολάς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.16.5, 1.24.4.}}
**II.4** **reject,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 888.15.}}
**II.5** **defer,** διήγησιν{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.1.}}
**II.6** **transfer,** ἔς τινας τὰς αἰτίας{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.66d.}}

644:
** ἀποσκῐ-ασμα**, ατος, τό,
**shadow,** ἀ. τροπῆς **a shadow cast by** turning, [{{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Jam.1.16, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “in Ptolemaei Tetrabiblon” 193, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {ἀνθήλιος}.}}]
**II** **illusion, deceit,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus Prot. p.118 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

645:
** ἀπο-σπάω**,
_future_ -σπάσω [ᾰ], **tear** or **drag away from,** τινός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1024, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 491b, etc.; ἀ. τινὰ ἀπὸ γυναικὸς καὶ τέκνων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1, compare 102; ἀποσπάσας.. περόνας ἀπ᾽ αὐτῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1268; μή μου τὸ τέκνον ἐκ χερῶν ἀποσπάσῃς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 277: rarely ἀ. τινά τι}} **tear** a thing **from** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 866}}; ἀ. τινά **tear** him **away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.91}}; ἀ. τι τῆς λείας **detach, abstract** some of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.26.8: _metaphorically_, ἀ. τινὰ ἐλπίδος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1432}}; and reversely also ἀ. τῆς φρενὸς αἵ μοι μόναι παρῆσαν ἐλπίδων{{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 809}}; **detach, withdraw,** πλήρωμα a gang of labourers, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.129 (3rd c.BC)}}; τινὰ ἀπό τινος{{NT+5th c.BC+|“BGU” 1125.9 (1st c.BC),compare below 5; μαθητάς NT.Act.20.30; ἀ. πολίτας τῆς θαλάσσης 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 19; ἀπὸ τοῦ φρονεῖν τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 962:—}}_middle_, τὴν μάχην οὕτω μακρὰν τῆς ναυτικῆς βοηθείας{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 76}}:—_passive_, **to be dragged away, detached, separated from,** τινός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.33, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 287, etc.; ἐξ ἱροῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.160; ἀπὸ τῶν ἱερῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.81}}; of a bone, **to be torn off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 13; ἀκρώμιον -σπασθέν [prev. author] “Mochl.” 6.}}
**2** ἀ. τινὰ κόμης **drag away** by the hair, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 909.}}
**3** ἀ. πύλας, θύρας, **tear off** the gates, doors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17, 3.159, etc.: _metaphorically_, πινακηδὸν ἀποσπῶν [ῥήματα] 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 824.}}
**4** ἀ. τὸ στρατόπεδον **draw off, divert** the army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.3.17: _absolutely_, ἀποσπάσας}}**having drawn off,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.2.11}}:—_passive_, of troops, **to become separated** or **broken,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.80, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.27.9.}}
**5** **withdraw, reclaim,** {{Refs|POxy. 496.9.}}
**6** ἀπεσπασμένος, ὁ, **eunuch,** {{LXX|LXX.Lev.22.24.}}
**II** _intransitive_ (i.e. ἑαυτόν), **separate** (i.e. **be separated**) **from,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.48, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 11, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 56.22; and in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.3 the best Mss. give πολὺ γὰρ ἀπέσπα (for ἀπέπτα) φεύγουσα [στρουθός].}}

646:
** ἀπο-στᾰσία**, ἡ,
late form for ἀπόστασις, **defection, revolt,** variant in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.1, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Vita” 10, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 1}}; especially in religious sense, **rebellion against God, apostasy,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Josh.22.22; NT.2Thes.2.3.}}
**2** **departure, disappearance,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 320.2.}}
**3** **distinguishing,** with _genitive_, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Elias Philosophus “in Aristotelis Categorias commentaria - pars” 119.7.}}
**4** **distance, ** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “ψαμμίτης” 1.5.}}

647:
Included with: ** ἀπο-στᾰσίου** δίκη
action against a freedman **for having forsaken his** προστάτης and chosen another, ἀ. ὀφλεῖν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.65, compare 35.48, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 58.3, probably in “IG” 2.776.}}
**II** ἀποστασίου βιβλίον writing or bill **of divorce,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.24.1, NT.Matt.19.7, NT.Mark.10.4: so ἀποστάσιον NT.Matt.5.32.}}
**III** ἀποστασίου συγγραφή deed **of cession, conveyance,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PHib. 1.96.3 (3rd c.BC),compare “PGiss.” 36.21 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; ἀντίγραφονίου{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 919.23 (2nd c.AD)}}; with συγγραφή omitted, especially in phrase πρᾶσις καὶ ἀποστασίου{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PTeb.” 561v (1st c.AD), etc.}}

648:
** ἀποστεγ-άζω**,
**uncover,** πυκινὸν ῥόον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 100.14(probably in 42.1)}}; ἀ. τὸν νεών **unroof** it, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.3.30, compare 4.4.6 (_passive_), “IG” 12(3).325.30 (Thera, 2nd c.AD, _passive_)}}; ἀ. τὸ τρῆμα **open** it, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Sotades Lyricus 2.}}
**2** **take off** a covering, τὴν στέγην{{NT|NT.Mark.2.4.}}
**II** ={ἀποστέγω} {{Refs|1}}**cover closely,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.6.5, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 924a37.}}

649:
** ἀποστέλλω**,
_future_ -στελῶ: _aorist_ ἀπέστειλα (see. below):— **send off** or **away from,** μή μ᾽.. τῆσδ᾽ ἀποστείλητε γῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 71, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 281; τῆσδ᾽ ἀ. χθονός [prev. author] “Cyc.” 468; ἔξω χθονός [prev. author] “Ph.” 485; ἐκ τῆς πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 607b}}; **send away, banish,** τὰ δίκαια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 450; τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 731}}:—_passive_, **go away, depart,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 115; ἀποστέλλου χθονός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 582; δόμων.. τῶν ἐμῶν ἀπεστάλης [prev. author] “Hel.” 660; φυγὰς ἀποσταλείς [prev. author] “Ph.” 319(Lyric poetry); πρὸς σὲ δεῦρ᾽ ἀπεστάλην [prev. author] “IT” 1409.}}
**II** **dispatch,** on some mission or service, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 125, 1297, etc.}}; frequently of messengers or forces, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.46, 123; νέας ἐπὶ χώρην [prev. author] 7.235, compare 8.64; στρατὸν παρά τινα [prev. author] 5.32; ναῦς αὐτοῖς ἀ. βοηθούς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.45: also ἀ. ἀποικίην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.150}}; οἰκιστάς (as a form of banishment), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1306b31; πρεσβείαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.28; ἀγγέλους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.5, etc.; ἀπαρχὴν εἰς Δελφούς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 485}}:—_passive_, with _infinitive_, οἱ ἀποσταλέντες στρατεύεσθαι{{Refs|[prev. author] 3.26, compare 5.33; ἀποσταλθέντες “GDI” 5186.4(Cretan dialect).}}
**III** **put off, doff,** θαἰμάτια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1084.}}
**IV** _intransitive_, **retire, withdraw,** of the sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.89}}; of persons, οἴκαδε{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.5.}}

650:
** ἀποστερ-έω·** —
_passive_, _future_ **-στερηθήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.70, variant in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.22}} also **-στερήσομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 137(Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.210; ἀποστεροῦμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.149:}}_perfect_ ἀπεστέρημαι, etc.:—**rob, despoil, defraud** one **of** a thing, with _accusative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, χρημάτων ἀ. τινά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92. ; τὸν πατέρα τῆς τυραννίδος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1605; τῆς ψυχῆς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.6: with _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, μή μ᾽ ἀποστερήσῃς ἡδονάν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1276(Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.9, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.43, etc.}}: _absolutely_, **commit fraud,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 487}}; ἀπεστερηκὼς ὑπ᾽ ἀνάγκης being constrained to **become a defaulter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241b; συνέστιον ὧν ἔκγονον ἢ ἀδελφὸν ἀπεστέρηκε γίγνεσθαι [prev. author] “Lg.” 868d}}:—_passive_, **to be robbed** or **deprived of,** with _genitive_, Ἑλλάδος ἀπεστερημένος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130; σοῦ τ᾽ ἀπεστερημένη καὶ πατρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 813; ἡδονῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1072; ἁπάντων ἂν ἀπεστερήμην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.106: with _accusative_, ἵππους ἀπεστέρηνται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.12, etc.: _absolutely_, εἰ δ᾽ ἀπεστερήμεθα}} if we **have been frustrated,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 782.}}
**2** ἀ. ἑαυτόν τινος **detach, withdraw** oneself **from** a person or thing, τῶν [ἀγαλμάτων].. ἀπεστέρησ᾽ ἐμαυτόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 1381; οὐκ ἀποστερῶν γε τῶν ἐς τὴν πόλιν ἑαυτὸν οὐδενός 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.78; ἄλλου ἑαυτὸν ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.40; ἀ. ἑαυτὸν τοῦ φρονεῖν 4th c.BC: Crobylus Comicus 3}}; ἐκείνους.. ἀ. μὴ ἂν.. ἀποτειχίσαι **deprive** them **of** the power of walling off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.6:—reversely, ἀ. τὸν ἔλεον ἑαυτοῦ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 26, compare “Dem.” 4.}}
**3** c._accusative_ _person_, **defraud, rob,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.155, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 373, NT.1Cor.6.7, etc.; θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 917d.}}
**4** with _accusative_ _of things_ only, **filch away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 931}}; **withhold,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 777, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 323, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1305}}; **refuse payment** of a debt, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.44, etc.}}; **refuse to give up,** παρακαταθήκην{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1383b21}}; Ζεὺς ἀποστεροίη γάμον **may** he **avert**it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1063(Lyric poetry).}}
**5** τὸ σαφές μ᾽ ἀποστερεῖ certainty **fails** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 577.}}
**II** in Logic, **draw a negative conclusion,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 44b23. (ἀποστέρω}} is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.243.)}}

651:
** ἀποστολ-ή**, ἡ,
(ἀποστέλλω) **sending off** or **away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 688, “Ph.” 1043(Lyric poetry, _plural_)}}; **dispatching,** νεῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.9}}; **sending forth on their journey,** ξένων ὑποδοχὰς καὶ ἀ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1123a3, compare “IG” 2.238.15; δοῦναί τι ἀποστολάς τινι}}**as a parting gift,** {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.9.16, cf. LXX.1Mac.2.18.}}
**2** **shooting, discharge,** βέλους{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 68.33.}}
**3** **discharge from service,** οὐκ ἔστιν ἀ. ἐν ἡμέρᾳ πολέμου{{LXX|LXX.Eccl.8.8.}}
**4** **payment** of tribute, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 204.}}
**II** (from _passive_) **expedition,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.8.}}
**II.2** **apostleship,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.9.2, NT.Gal.2.8.}}

652:
** ἀπόστολ-ος**, ὁ,
**messenger, ambassador, envoy,** ὁ μὲν δὴ ἀ. ἐς τὴν Μίλητον ἦν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.21}}; ἐς Λακεδαίμονα τριήρεϊ ἀ. ἐγίνετο he went off **on a mission** to Laced., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.38.}}
**b** **commander of a naval force,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**2** **messenger from God,** {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.14.6}}; especially of the **Apostles,** {{NT|NT.Matt.10.2, +others}}
**II** ={στόλος}, **naval squadron** or **expedition,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.21}}; ἀπόστολον ἀφιέναι, ἀποστέλλειν, ποιεῖσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.5, 18.80,107, “IG” 2.809b190.}}
**II.2** **colony,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.59.}}
**II.3** ={ἀποστολή}, of envoys, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.11.1.}}
**II.4** ἀπόστολον, τό, with or without πλοῖον, **packet,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 346a, Pseudo-Herodotus Historicus “Vitae Homeri” 19.}}
**II.5** ἀπόστολος, ὁ, **order for dispatch,** of a vessel, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPHerm.” 6.11 (3rd c.AD, _plural_), “PAmh.” 2.138.10(4th c.AD),compare “Digesta” 49.6.1.}}
**II.6** **export-licence,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PGnom. 162 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.7** _genitive_ uncertain, **cargo dispatched by order,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 522.1, +others (2nd c.AD), “PTeb.” 486 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}

653:
** ἀποστομ-ᾰτίζω**,
(στόμα) **teach by word of mouth, teach by dictation,** γράμματα ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 277a: _absolutely_, ὅταν ἀ. ὑμῖν ὁ γραμματεύς [prev. work] 276c:}} —_passive_, τὸ ἀποστοματιζόμενον **dictated lesson,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. passage], 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 165b32.}}
**2** **interrogate, catechize,** as a master his pupil, {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Luke.11.53, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus cited in 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.102 (_passive_).}}
**II** **repeat by heart,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.359d, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.9.4}}: generally, **recite, repeat,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 24.}}

654:
** ἀπο-στρέφω**,
_Doric dialect_ _aorist_ ἀποστράψαι{{Refs|“SIG” 244 ii 16(Delph.)}}; _Ionic dialect_ _aorist_ ἀποστρέψασκε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.197, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἀπέστροφα{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.6.21}}:—_passive_ and _middle_, _future_ **-στρέψομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.36, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.387c:}}_aorist_ -εστράφην [ᾰ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|], 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1272, etc.}} later **-εστρεψάμην** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Hos.8.3, probably in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 776}}: _future_ **-στρᾰφήσομαι**{{LXX|LXX.Num.25.4, +others}}: _perfect_ **-έστραμμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.166, etc.: _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_}}_pluperfect_ -εστράφατο{{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; **-έστρεμμαι**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.392.11 (3rd c.BC)}}: —**turn back**: hence, either **turn to flight,** ὄφρ᾽.. Ἀχαιοὺς αὖτις ἀποστρέψῃσιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.62, etc., compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.94}}; or **turn back from flight,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.1}}; **send home again,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.97, 5.75}}; ῥῆμα **bring back** word, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.22.9}}; ἀποστρέψαντε πόδας καὶ χεῖρας **having twisted back** the hands and feet so as to bind them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.173, 190,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1154; τὸν ὦμον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 263; ἀποστρέφετε τὰς χεῖρας αὐτῶν, ὦ Σκύθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 455; ἀ. τὸν αὐχένα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.188}}; **guide back again,** ἀποστρέψαντες ἔβαν νέας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.162}}; ἴχνι᾽ ἀποστρέψας **having turned** the steps of the oxen **backwards** so as to make it appear that they had gone the other way, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 76}}; **turn away, avert,** αὐχέν᾽ ἀποστρέψας{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 858; ἀπέστρεψ᾽ ἔμπαλιν παρηΐδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1148; but τὸ πρόσωπον πρός τινα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 6}}; **bring back, recall,** ἐξ ἰσθμοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.3}}; φῶτας ἀπέστρεψεν Περσεφόνης θαλάμων [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus] 156.4.}}
**2** **turn away** or **aside, divert,** variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.80, etc.}}; ὕδατα **cut off** water from a besieged town, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 97.4; τὸν Κάϋστρον “SIG” 839.14 (from Ephesus)}}; τὸν πόλεμον ἐς Μακεδονίαν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.1}}; **avert** a danger, an evil, etc., πῆμ᾽ ἀ. νόσου{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 850 (from Porson)}}; **prevent,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.136}}; **rebut,** δίκην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 776(see. above); ἀ. τύχην μὴ οὐ γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.15 codices; ἀ. εἰς τοὐναντίον τοὺς λόγους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 239d; τὰς πράξεις εἰς τοὺς ἀντιδίκους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1442b6.}}
**3** ἀ. τινά τινος **dissuade from,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.12; τινὰ ἀπὸ τοῦ λήμματος 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.23; πότων ἀ. τοὺς στομάχους 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 15.}}
**II** as if _intransitive_ (i.e. ἑαυτόν, ἵππον, ναῦν, etc.), **turn back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.65; ἀ. ὀπίσω 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.43; ἀ. πάλιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1403.}}
**II.2** **turn away** or **aside,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.87}}; of a river, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.52; τἀναντία ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.12.}}
**B** _passive_, **to be turned back,** ἀπεστράφθαι τοὺς ἐμβόλους, of ships, **to have** their beaks **bent back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.166}}; ἀποστραφῆναι.. τὼ πόδε **to have** one's feet **twisted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 279; τρίχες ἀπεστραμμέναι}}**closecurled,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 809b26.}}
**B.II** _middle_ and _passive_, **turn oneself from** or **away,** ἀπεστραμμέναι ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων{{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 611a6; ἀπεστραμμένοι}}**back to back,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 145.2: especially}}
**B.II.1** **turn one's face away from, abandon,** with _accusative_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus 2, 4th c.AD: Sallustius Philosophus 3; ἐχθροῦ ἀξίωσιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 215; μή μ᾽ ἀποστραφῇς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1272; μή μ᾽ ἀποστρέφου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 801, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 683, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.36, “PSI” [same place]; τὸ θεῖον ῥᾳδίως ἀπεστράφης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 159: also with _genitive_, ἄψορρος οἴκων τῶνδ᾽ ἀποστραφείς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 431: with _dative_, ἀστεφανώτοισι ἀπυστρέφονται 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 78: _absolutely_, μὴ πρὸς θεῶν.. ἀποστραφῇς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 326}}; ἀπεστραμμένοι λόγοι **hostile** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.160; τὴν διάνοιαν ἀποστρέφεσθαι}}**to be alienated,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.80.}}
**B.II.2** **turn oneself about,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.25; ἅρματα ἀπεστραμμένα ὥσπερ εἰς φυγήν [prev. work] 6.2.17}}; ἀποστραφῆναι λυγιζόμενος **escape** by wriggling, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 405c.}}
**B.II.3** ἀποστραφῆναί τινος **fall off from** one, **desert** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.4.}}

657:
** ἀποτάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἀποτάττω**, **set apart, assign specially,** χώραν τινί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153e}}; **detach** soldiers, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.35.3, etc., compare “POxy.” 475.27 (2nd c.AD):}} — _passive_, ἀποτεταγμένη ἀρχή **distinct** office, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1322a26; ἀποτεταγμένοι τῇ κατοικίᾳ χρηματισταί “PFay.” 12 (2nd c.BC)}}: generally, **to be fixed, appointed,** χῶρος{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.120b}}; **ear-mark,** ἀργύριον εἰς δημοθοινίαν -τεταγμένον{{Refs|“IG” 12(7).515.91 (from Amorgos), compare “BCH” 46.397 (from Mylasa).}}
**II** **appoint, settle definitely,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 585b36.}}
**III** **remove, exclude,** τόπον τοῦ κόσμου{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.35; ἑαυτόν τινων [prev. author] “Im.” 2.19.}}
**IV** Med, ἀποτάσσομαί τινι **bid adieu to** a person, **part from** them, {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Luke.9.61, NT.Act.18.21, NT.Mark.6.46, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.8.6, “BGU” 884 ii 14(2nd-3rd c.AD), 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 64, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Declamationes” 45.28}}; **have done with, get rid of** a person, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 298.31 (1st c.AD)}}; ἀ. τῷ βίῳ **commit suicide,** {{Refs|Cat.Codex Astr. 8 (3). 136.17}}: also with _dative_ _of things_, **renounce, give up,** τοῖς ἑαυτοῦ ὑπάρχουσιν{{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.14.33; τροφῇ 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.6.8}}; ταῖς μίξεσι, of the Vestals, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.32; πάθεσι 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.116, compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 3.13, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 15.}}

658:
** ἀποτελέω**,
_future_ -τελέσω, _Attic dialect_ -τελῶ, **bring to an end, complete** a work, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.ή, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 308e, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.69:}}_perfect_ _participle_ ἀποτετελεσμένος **perfect,** ἐπίτροπος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 13.3.}}
**2** **produce,** νοσήματα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 84c, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.20U., 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 10.26, etc.}}: —_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 268b26.}}
**3** **pay** or **perform what is due,** τὰς εὐχάς σφι ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.65; τῷ θεῷ τὰ πάτρια [prev. author] 4.180}}; of rent or tribute, τὰ νομιζόμενα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.19; ἀπαρχὴν τῶν ἐκ τῆς γῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 806e}}; **pay** or **suffer,** παραπλήσια τοῖς Καμβύσου παθήμασιν {{Refs|[prev. work] 695e.}}
**4** **accomplish, perform,** τὰ καθήκοντα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.5; προσταχθέντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 823d; τὰ προσήκοντα [prev. author] “Criti.” 108d}}; ἀ. ἄρτον **accomplish the making of** bread, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 3; ἓν ὑφ᾽ ἑνὸς ἕργον ἄριστ᾽ ἀποτελεῖται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1273b10.}}
**4.b** especially of astral influences, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 1, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 45.1, etc.}}; compare ἀποτέλεσμα. with Astrology texts, **make a forecast,** περὶ ζωῆς Ps. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Centil.” 215.}}
**5** **render** of a certain kind, τὴν πόλιν ἀ. εὐδαίμονα **make** the state **quite** happy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 718b; ἀμείνους ἐκ χειρόνων ἀ. [prev. author] “Plt.” 297b, compare “Lg.” 823d; τοιούτους ἄνδρας ὥστε.. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.52.11:}} —in _middle_, ἄμεμπτον φίλον ἀποτελέσασθαι **make** him without blame **towards himself.** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 2.13}}:— _passive_, τύραννος ἀντὶ προστάτου ἀποτετελεσμένος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 566d}}; ἐνύπνιον τέλεον ἀ. **turns out..,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 443b.}}
**6** **fill up, satiate,** τὰς ἐπιθυμίας{{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 503d}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 558e, +others}}
**II** _passive_, **to be worshipped,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 188d.}}

659:
** ἀποτίθημι**,
**put away, stow away,** δέπας δ᾽ ἀπέθηκ᾽ ἐνὶ χηλῷ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.254, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.15; ἀ. εἰς δεσμωτήριον 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 112}}: _metaphorically_, 'pigeon-hole', **class,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 361.22.}}
**2** **expose** a child, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161a.}}
**II** _middle_ (_aorist_ I _participle_ ἀποθησαμένη{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}, **put away from oneself, lay aside,** τεύχεα κάλ᾽ ἀποθέσθαι ἐπὶ χθονί{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.89}}; τὴν Σκυθικὴν στολὴν ἀ. **put** it **off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.78}}; ἀ. κόμας **cut** it **off,** in mourning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 367 (Lyric poetry, tmesis)}}; ἀ. τὸν νόμον **set aside,** i.e. **disregard,** the law, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77}}; ἀ. τὰν Ἀφροδίταν **quell** desire, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 558 (Lyric poetry); ἀ. ῥᾳθυμίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.8, 8.46; ὀργήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 19; ἀρχήν [prev. author] “Pomp.” 23.}}
**II.2** **put away from oneself, avoid,** ἀποθέσθαι ἐνιπήν **wipe away** the reproach, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.492, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 762; νόστον ἔχθιστον ἀπεθήκατο 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.68, compare 10(11).40.}}
**II.3** **put by for oneself, stow away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1219, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.15; ἀ. τροφὴν τοῖς νεοττοῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 619a20; ἀ. τινὰ εἰς φυλακήν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.10.8}}; frequently of drugs, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.136, +others, compare “PEleph.” 12 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐν φυλακῇ{{NT|NT.Matt.14.3.}}
**II.3.b** **bury,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.1974.}}
**II.4** ἀποτίθεσθαι εἰς αὖθις **put off, defer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 376, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 449b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.7, etc.; εἰς τοὺς παῖδας ἀ. τὰς τιμωρίας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 53.3.}}
**II.5** **reserve, keep back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 887c, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.30.}}
**II.6** ἀπεθήκατο κόλπων, of a woman, **laid down the burden of** her womb, i.e. bore a child, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 25; ἀ. ὠδῖνας 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.5.2: but}}
**II.7** μηδὲν ἀποτίθεσθαι τῶν γιγνομένων **expose** none of **one's** children, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335b22.}}
**II.8** ἀ. χρόνον εἴς τι **employ, bestow** time upon it, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.9.10.}}
**II.9** **set** a fracture, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus}}**in Hp.Fract.**{{Refs|12.276}} C.; compare ἀπόθεσις.

661:
** ἀποτίνω**,
{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἀπυτείω “IG” 5(2).6.43 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}_future_ -τείσω: _aorist_ -έτεισ; Thess. _3rd.pers.singular_ _aorist_ _imperative_ ἀππεισάτον{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work]9(2).1229 (2nd c.BC)}}:— **repay,** τιμὴν δ᾽ Ἀργείοις ἀποτινέμεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.286; εὐεργεσίας ἀποτίνειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.235.}}
**2** **pay for** a thing, πρὶν.. μνηστῆρας ὑπερβασίην σίην ἀποτεῖσαι {{Refs|[prev. work] 13.193}}; Πατρόκλοιο δ᾽ ἕλωρα.. ἀποτείσῃ **may atone for** making a prey of Patroclus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.93}}; σύν τε μεγάλῳ ἀπέτεισαν **made atonement** with a great price,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.161; ἀ. αἷμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1338 (Lyric poetry); πληγὰς τῶν ὑπεραύχων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1352.}}
**3** more frequently, **pay in full,** τίσιν οὐκ ἀποτείσει Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.56, compare 3.109; ζημίην [prev. author] 2.65; ἀργύριον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1255; ἐγγύας 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.12, compare 5.63; χρήματα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.29; ἀξίαν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 30.1}}; ἀπότεισον **pay** the wager! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1059}}; in Law παθεῖν ἢ ἀποτεῖσαι are opposed to denote personal or pecuniary penalties, e.g. Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.47, compare [prev. work]25; τί ἄξιός εἰμι παθεῖν ἢ ἀ.; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36b, compare “Plt.” 299a, “Lg.” 843b, +others}}
**4** with _accusative_ _person_, ἀλάστωρ.. τόνδ᾽ ἀπέτεισεν **made** him **the price,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1503 (Lyric poetry).}}
**5** τὸ πεπρωμένον ἀ. **pay the debt of** fate, i.e. **die,** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 509 (Thess.).}}
**II** _middle_, ἀποτίνομαι, _poetry_ ἀποτίνῠμαι (frequently written -τίννυμαι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.398, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 247 (assuming variant), 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 362, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.65:}}_future_ -τείσομαι:—**to get paid one, exact** or **require** a penalty **from,** πόλεων δ᾽ ἀπετίνυτο ποινήν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.398, etc.: with double _accusative_, ἀποτείσασθαι δίκην ἐχθρούς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 852; δέκα τάλαντ᾽ ἀ. 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 317 (uncertain), etc.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _person_, ἀποτείσασθαί τινα **avenge oneself on** another, **punish** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.35, etc.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **take vengeance for** a thing, **punish** it, εἴ κέ ποτέ σφι βίας ἀποτείσεται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.216, compare 16.255; τὰ παράνομα.. θεὸς ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 684: with _genitive_ _of things_, ἀ. τῶν.. ἱρῶν κατακαυθέντων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.101, see above 1.2}}: _absolutely_, **take vengeance,** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus [same place], 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.16. [ἀποτίνω] has ῑ by position before νϝ in _Epic dialect_, ῐ in _Attic dialect_ For ἀποτίνυμαι, which has ῑ by nature, ἀποτείνυμαι should perhaps be read in early texts; compare {ἀποτίνυμι}]}}]

663:
** ἀποτομ-ία**, ἡ,
**severity,** νόμων{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.16, “POxy.” 237 vii 40 (1st c.AD)}}; ἐπιτιμημάτων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.13d; ἀναβάσεως “BGU” 1208.16 (Aug.); φαλάριδος 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 292; περὶ τὰ δίκαια 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.61}}; of tortures, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.287; καῦσις διὰ τὴν ἀ. 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus (?) cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.35.}}
**II** **cutting off,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Demosthenes Ophthalmicus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.81.}}
**III** **sheer madness,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 2.12.}}

664:
Included with: ** ἀποτοξεύω**,
**shoot off arrows,** ἀπὸ δένδρων{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.2}}: _metaphorically_, **shoot off like an arrow,** ῥηματίσκια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 180a, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 17}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prom.Es” 2.}}
**2** **shoot** a person, τινά τινι{{Refs|[prev. author] “Vit.Auct.” 24 (codices, κατατοξ- Cobet).}}
**II** **keep off by shooting,** λοιμόν{{Refs|[prev. author] “Alex.” 36.}}

665:
** ἀποτρέπ-ω**,
**turn away from,** εἰ δὲ σὺ.. τιν᾽ ἄλλον.. ἀποτρέψεις πολέμοιο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.249, compare 20.256; ὅθεν.. ἀπέτραπε λαὸν Ἀθήνη 11.758}}; **deter** or **dissuade from,** τινός τινα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39; τινὰ τῆς κακουργίας [prev. author] 6.38; τῆς γνώμης 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.21, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, ἀ. προσωτέρω τὸ μὴ πορεύεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.105; ἀ. βοᾶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supp..” 900 (Lyric poetry); δηλοῦν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.26, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.7.5, 6}}: with _participle_, ἀ. τινὰ ὑβρίζοντα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 880}}:—_passive_, ὁ παραβαίνειν τι βουλόμενος τῷ μὴ προὔχων ἂν ἐπελθεῖν -τρέπεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.11, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 19.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_ only, **turn away** or **back,** πάντας ἀπέτραπε καὶ μεμαῶτας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.276: with _dative_ modi, οὔ μ᾽ ἐπέεσσιν ἀποτρέψεις μεμαῶτα 20.256, compare 109}}; τοὺς ἀλαζόνας ἀ. **deter** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 173c}}; opposed to παροξῦναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.37}}; opposed to συμβουλεύω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1391b33, etc.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **turn back again,** ποτὶ χέρσον ἔντεα ναός{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.69.}}
**4** **turn aside, avert,** ἀπὸ δὲ.. ἔγχεος ὁρμὴν ἔτραπε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 455}}; **pervert,** δίκας κέλευθον ὀρθᾶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.27}}; τὸ σφάλμα ἀ. **prevent, avert** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207; τὸ μέλλον γενέσθαι [prev. author] 3.65, compare 8.29, +others}}; ἀ. βλάβην, συμφοράν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 509b, “Phdr.” 231d}}; ἀ. τὴν εἰρήνην **prevent** its being made, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.12.}}
**5** **turn from** others **against** one, ἐπὶ τῷδε.. οὐκ ἔγχος τις.. ἀποτρέψε; see reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1013 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, ἀποτετράφθαι πρὸς τὴν ἄλλην Ἰταλίαν{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 19:}} —_middle_, ἀποτραπόμενος πρὸς θυσίαν, i.e. **turning away from other objects to** this one, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Rom.” 7; εἰς τὴν μεσογείαν -τραπόμενος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 52.}}
**II** _middle_ and (later) _passive_, **turn from, desist from,** with _participle_, ἀπετράπετ᾽ ὄβριμος Ἕκτωρ ὀλλὺς Ἀργείους{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.200}}: with _infinitive_, λέγειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 410, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 23 (b); ἀ. ἐκ κινδύνων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40; ἀ. τοῦ ἐρωτήματος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.13.}}
**II.2** **turn away, turn a deaf ear,** οὐδέ.. ἀπετράπετ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἀπίθησεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.329: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 206d.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **turn away from, shrink from,** δεῖμα πολιτῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1065 (_anapaest meter_); τἀληθές 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 336 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68, and late Prose, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cleomenes” 9, etc.}}
**II.4** **turn back, return,** ἐπ᾽ ὄκου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.13; ἐς τὴν πόλιν [prev. author] 3.24; ἀποτρεπόμενοι ἵεντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.13.}}
**II.5** **dissuade, deter,** τινά{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.13.1.}}
**II.6** **beat off, repulse,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 42.}}

666:
** ἀπουσία**, ἡ,
(ἀπεῖναι) **absence,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1259, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 962, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70, NT.Phil.2.12, etc.}}
**II** **waste,** as in smelting ore, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 383b3, 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 28; τρῖψις καὶ ἀ. “POxy.” 1273.32 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** ={ἀποσπερματισμός}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.364d.}}

667:
** ἀποφέρω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in}}_future_ -οίσω (_Doric dialect_
**A** -οισῶ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 779}}, _middle_ -οίσομαι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.3, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 33) and _Ionic dialect_}}_aorist_ ἀπένεικα: _Attic dialect_ _aorist_ **-ήνεγκα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.10}}: _aorist 2_ **-ήνεγκον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 582, etc.}}: _perfect_ **-ενήνοχα**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.20}}:—**carry off** or **away,** τεύχεα δέ σφ᾽ ἀπένεικαν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.360, etc.}}; of a wind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.255, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.179: _metaphorically_, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.374e}}; of a disease, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.66, 6.27; generally, ἀ. σῆμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 614; βρέφος ἐς ἄντρον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 16, cf. NT.Mark.15.1, etc.}}:—_passive_, **to be carried from one's course,** ὑπ᾽ ἀνέμων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.114, compare 116; ἀπενεχθέντες ἐς Αιβύην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.50, compare 6.104.}}
**A.2** **exhale, evaporate,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Anonymus Londinensis 22.25}}:—_passive_, **to be wafted,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.681a.}}
**A.II** **carry** or **bring back,** αὖτις ἀποίσετον ὠκέες ἵπποι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.257; ἂψ Ἕκτορι μῦθον ἀποίσειν 10.337; ἀ. οἴκαδις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 779}}: —so in _passive_, of oracles, ταῦτα ἀπενειχθέντα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.66, 158, 160}}: but in _passive_, also of persons, **return,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.164, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, etc.}}; ἀπηνέχθη εἰς.. ἔτι ζῶν **was carried home,** of a sick man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.1; τεθνεὼς ἐκ δεσμωτηρίου ἀ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.18.}}
**A.II.2** **pay back, return,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196, etc.}}: hence, **pay what is due** as tribute, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.35, 5.84, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.31.}}
**A.II.3** **bring in, return,** of slaves let out to labour for their master's profit, variant {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.97, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 2.}}
**A.II.4** generally, **bring, hand over as required,** τί τινι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; ὅπλα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.34; εἰς τὰ δημόσια ἀ. ἱερὰ τὰ ἴδια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 910c.}}
**A.III** **hand in** an accusation, **render** accounts, returns, etc., ἀ. παρανόμων (i.e. γραφήν) πρὸς τὸν ἄρχοντα Docum. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.54, compare 52.30}}; ἀπήνεγκε παρανόμων (i.e. γραφήν) Δημοσθένει Canon Laws texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.105; λόγον.. ἀπενήνοχεν ἀναλωμάτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.20}}; λόγον πρὸς τοὺς λογιστάς, λόγον τῇ πόλει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.22; ἀ. τοὺς ἱππεύσαντας}}**hand in a list of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.6; ναύτας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.6}}; ἀ. ἐν τῷ λόγῳ δεδωκώς **having entered** in the account, {{Refs|[prev. author] 49.16}}:—_passive_, **to be returned as** so and so, ἀπηνέχθη ἀνώμοτος{{Refs|[prev. author] 21.86; διαιτητὴς ἀπενηνεγμένος [prev. author] 52.30.}}
**A.III.2** **deliver** a letter, {{Refs|[prev. author] 34.8.}}
**A.IV** **bring home, receive as wages,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 12}} (which others refer to _signification_ {{Refs|11.2).}}
**B** _middle_, **take away with one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.132, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.74, etc.}}; **carry off** a prize, μετὰ Πᾶνα τὸ δεύτερον ἆθλον ἀποισῇ Theoc.[same place]; κάλλευς πρῶτ᾽ ἀπενεγκαμέναν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“APl.” 4.166 (5th c.BC: Evenus Elegiacus); ἀ. δόξαν 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.5.7}}; **carry home** delicacies from a banquet, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 38}} (less frequently in _active_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nigr.” 25).}}
**B.2** **take for oneself, gain, obtain,** λέχη ἀλλότρια{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1089 codices}}; **receive to oneself,** μόρον{{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 595.}}
**B.3** **obtain** a decision, **win** a lawsuit, δίκην κενὴν θελόντων ἀ.{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 111.150 (1st c.BC).}}
**B.II** **bring back for oneself,** ὀπίσω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.152; ἀ. σημεῖα τοῦ θυμῷ μάχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 6.2}}; ἀ. βίον μητρί, i.e. **return to** her alive, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1161; νόστον [prev. author] “IA” 298 (Lyric poetry).}}
**C** Intr. in _active_, **be off,** ἀπόφερ᾽ ἐς κόρακας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1221.}}

668:
** ἀπο-φεύγω**,
_future_ **-φεύξομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39a}} **-οῦμαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 932}}: _perfect_ **-πέφευγα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.9, etc.}}:—**flee from, escape,** c._accusative_, {{NT+6th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 42, 47; σοφίην ὁ σοφώτατος οὐκ ἀποφεύγει 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1159; τὴν πεπρωμένην μοῖραν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91; τὴν μάχην [prev. author] 5.102; κῆρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1166 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30a; νόσον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.15; ἀ. ἐκ τῶν πλησίον κωμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.9; ἐς Νίσαιαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.114: rarely with _genitive_, ἀ. τῆς φθορᾶς NT.2Pet.1.4}}: with _infinitive_, **auoid,** λέγειν{{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 617.14}}: _absolutely_, **get safe away, escape,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, 9.102, etc.}}; **go free,** of manumitted slaves, {{Refs|“IG” 2.786, +others}}
**II** as law-term, ἀ. πολλὸν τοὺς διώκοντας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.82; τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.123; φεύγων ἂν ἀποφύγοι δίκην 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 167, compare 1151; γραφάς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.16; εὐθύνας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 946d: with double _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, ἀπέφυγον αὐτοὺς τὰς δίκας ἅς μοι ἐνεκάλουν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.19.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **get clear off, be acquitted,** opposed to ἁλίσκομαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.174, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.103; κἂν.. εἰσέλθῃ φεύγων οὐκ ἀποφεύγει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 579.}}
**II.3** of a woman in child-birth, **bring to birth,** ἀ. τὸ παιδίον ἐν τῷ τόκῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.25; also ἀ. τοῦ παιδίου [prev. work] 33: _intransitive_, ἢν τὰ ὕστερα μὴ δύνηται ἀποφυγεῖν [prev. author] “Nat.Mul.” 56.}}

669:
** ἀπο-φθέγγομαι**,
**speak one's opinion plainly,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Zeux.” 1}}; **utter an apophthegm,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.405e, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 11.55; χρησμόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 25, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.27, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 73.24}}: _metaphorically_ of vessels when struck, **ring,** σαπρὸν ἀ.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 4.}}
**II** **chant hymns,** {{Refs|LXX 1 “Chr.” 25.1.}}

671:
** ἀπό-χρησις**, εως, ἡ,
**getting rid of,** τῶν περιττῶν uncertain in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.267f.}}
**II** **consumption, using up,** {{NT|NT.Col.2.22.}}

672:
** ἀποχωρ-έω**,
_future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.13, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.78}}:—**go from** or **away from,** δόμων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 456; ἐκ τοῦ στρατοπέδου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 394a.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **depart,** πάλιν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 265}}; **withdraw,** ἐπὶ τὰ ἀναγκαῖα variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.36}}; especially after a defeat, **retire, retreat,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89, etc.; πρὸς τὴν πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.11; ἐπὶ τῆς Κορίνθου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.94.}}
**3** ἀ.ἐκ τῶν πόλεων **.. withdraw from.., give up possession of,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.13.}}
**3.b** _metaphorically_, **withdraw** or **dissent from** opinions, δοξῶν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.356, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.1.53.}}
**4** **turn out, succeed,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.105 S}}.; of persons, κατὰ τρόπον ἀ. **to be successful,** {{Refs|[prev. work]2.259S.}}
**5** **have recourse,** εἴς, ἐπί τι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.78.37.21.}}
**II** **pass off,** especially of the excretions of the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ κρισίων” 10, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.16}}; τὰ ἀποχωροῦντα **excrements,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.4.6}}; τὸ ἀποχωροῦν **excretion,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 725b15.}}
**III** of places, **to be distant,** μέρη ἀποκεχωρηκότα{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.27.8}}; ἀ. ὡς πόδα **to be** a foot **apart,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 165.1.}}

673:
** ἀποχωρ-ίζω**,
**separate from,** τὸ χεῖρον ἀπὸ βελτίονος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 226d}}:—_passive_, **to be separated from,** πυρός{{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 59d; ἐξ ἰνῶν αἷμα ἀ. [prev. work] 84a.}}
**2** **separate, set apart, detach,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.16}}; ἀ. ὡς ἓν εἶδος **separate and put** into one class, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 262e; ἀπὸ βασιλικῆς τε καὶ πολιτικῆς πράξεως [prev. work] 289d.}}
**3** _passive_, **to be vomited,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus}} _middle_ in {{Refs|“Rh.Mus.” 58.99.}}

674:
** ἀπο-ψύχω** [ῡ]:—
_passive_, _aorist_ ἀπεψύχθην and ἀπεψύχην [ῠ], see below, also ἀπεψύγην [ῠ] {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.3}}:— **leave off breathing, faint, swoon,** τὸν δὲ.. εἷλεν ἀποψύχοντα{{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.348; ἀ. ἀπὸ φόβου NT.Luke.21.26.}}
**2** with _accusative_, ἀπέψυξεν βίον **breathed out** life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1031; πνεῦμα “Anthologia Graeca” 12.72 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus)}}: _absolutely_, **expire, die,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.134, cf. LXX.4Mac.15.17, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 43.11, +others}}; λεπτὸν ἀ. faintly **breathing out his life,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.9}}:—also _passive_, ἀποψύχεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.19}}: _aorist 2_ ἀπεψύχη{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 104.}}
**II** **cool, chill,** ὄψα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 54}}:—_passive_ or _middle_, **to be cooled,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in phrase ἱδρῶ ἀπεψύχοντο χιτώνων στάντε ποτὶ πνοιήν}} they **got** the sweat **dried off** their tunics, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.621; ἱδρῶ ἀποψυχθείς (by bathing) 21.561}} (also in _active_, ἱδρῶ ἀποψύχοντε{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 1091)}}: generally, **grow cold,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.7.3, etc.: _metaphorically_, ἀπεψυγμένοι πρὸς τὸ μέλλον}} **cold and indifferent** as to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1383a4; ἀποψυχόμενοι}}**shivering with terror,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.1.145, compare 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.2}}; but, **to be refreshed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.27 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II.2** _impersonal_, ἀποψύχει **it grows cool,** ἐπειδὰν ἀποψύχῃ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242a, cited in 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.45 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, sed to be read ἀποψυχῇ (_aorist 2_ _passive_).}}
**III** ἀποψύχειν· ἀποπατεῖν, ἀφοδεύειν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; compare ἀπόψυγμα.

677:
** ἀπρό-σκοπος**, ον,
**B** **unseeing,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 105.}}
**B.II** **unexplored,** ὁδός{{LXX|LXX.Sir.35(32).21.}}

679:
** ἄπταιστος**, ον,
**not stumbling,** ἀπταιστστερον παρέχειν τὸν ἵππον make a horse **less aptto stumble,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.6: _metaphorically_, ἄ ἐν τῷ βίῳ 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Gnomologium” 52; δώμασιν ἀ. 2nd c.BC: Limenius Lyricus 43, compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 5.9; βίος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 46}}; **infallible,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.3.17, 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Metaphysica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 713.12; ἀλήθεια 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.31; θεοὶ διδασκάλων-ότατοι 2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 38.1}}. _adverb_ **-τως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 144b}}; **inevitably,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.230}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον** **with greater precision,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 177}}:—also **ἀπρο-τί**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Epim.” 256.}}
**2** **intact,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.691d.}}
**II** **not causing to stumble, giving a good footing,** λεία καὶ ἄ. ὁδός{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus Tyr. 5.2.}}

680:
Included with: ** ἅπτω**,
_future_ ἅψω: _aorist_ ἧψα:—_passive_, _perfect_ ἧμμαι, _Ionic dialect_ ἅμμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86}}: _future_ ἅψομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.379, ἁφθήσομαι}} (συν-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 3.311:}} —_middle_, see.below (compare ἑάφθη):—**fasten** or **bind to,** used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, once in}}_active_, ἅψας ἀμφοτέρωθεν.. ἔντερον οἰός (of a lyre-string) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.408}}; once in _middle_, ἁψαμένη βρόχον.. ἀφ᾽ ὑψηλοῖο μελάθρου **having fastened** the noose to the beam (to hang herself), {{Refs|11.278}}; so later ἅψεται ἀμφὶ βρόχον.. δείρᾳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 770; ἁψαμένη βρόχον αὐχένι 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1065}}:—_active_, βρόχους ἅ. κρεμαστούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1036; but βρόχῳ ἅ. δέρην [prev. author] “Hel.” 136, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 7.493 (Antip. Thess.).}}
**2** **join,** ἅ. χορόν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 307}}; πάλην τινὶ ἅ. **fasten** a contest in wrestling **on** one, **engage with** one, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 868}}: —_passive_, ἅπτεσθαι τὴν Μεγαρέων πόλιν καὶ Κορινθίων τοῖς τείχεσιν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280b14.}}
**II** more frequently in _middle_, ἅπτομαι, _future_ ἅψομαι, _aorist_ ἡψάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 317}}, with _perfect_ _passive_ ἧμμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1010(Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260e}}:—**fasten oneself to, grasp,** c._genitive_, ἅψασθαι γούνων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.512; χειρῶν 10.377; ἁψαμένη δὲ γενείου Ὀδυσσῆα προσέειπεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.473; ἅπτεσθαι νηῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.152; βρώμης δ᾽ οὐχ ἅπτεαι οὐδὲ ποτῆτο; 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.379, compare 4.60; ὡς δ᾽ ὅτε τίς τε κύων συὸς.. ἅπτηται κατόπισθε.. ἰσχία τε γλουτούς τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.339; ἅπτεσθαι τοῦ ἐπεόντος ἐπὶ τῶν δενδρέων καρποῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32; τῶν τύμβων ἁπτόμενοι [prev. author] 4.172}}; ἅπτεσθαί τινος, Latin _manus inicere alicui,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.137; οὔτ᾽ ἔθιγεν οὔθ᾽ ἥψαθ᾽ ἡμῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 617}}; τῶν σφυγμῶν **feel** the pulse, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.73}}: _metaphorically_, **take hold of, cleave to,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 967c.}}
**II.b** _absolutely_, τῶν μὲν γὰρ πάντων βέλε᾽ ἅπτεται for the spears of all the Trojans **reach their mark,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.631; ἀμφοτέρων βέλε᾽ ἥπτετο 8.67.}}
**II.c** ἅ. τῆς γῆς **land,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.48.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **engage in, undertake,** βουλευμάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 179; ἀγῶνος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 317}}; πολέμου **prosecute** it **vigorously,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.61; ἧπται τοῦ πράγματος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.155; ψυχὴ ἡμμένη φόνων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 108b, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 381; τῶν μεγίστων ἀσεβημάτων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.13.6; so ἅ. τῆς μουσικῆς καὶ φιλοσοφίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 411c}}; ἐπιτηδεύματος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 497e; γεωμετρίας [prev. author] “Plt.” 266a; τῆς θαλάττης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.24.7; ἅπτεσθαι λόγου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 662, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 283a (but ἅπτεσθαι τοῦ λόγου}} **attack, impugn** the argument of another, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 86d)}}; τούτων ἥψατο **touched on** these points, **handled** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97; ἅ. τῆς ζητήσεως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 320b34}}; but also, **touch on, treat superficially,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 694c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1227a1.}}
**III.b** _absolutely_, **begin, set to work,** ταῖς διανοίαις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 581.}}
**III.2** **fasten upon, attack,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.22, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1608, etc.; μόνον τῷ δακτύλῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 365; τῆς οὐραγίας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.34.12; especially with words, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.γ}}; of diseases, ἧπταί μου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1010, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.702; ἥψατο τῶν ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.48}}; ὅσα ἅπτεται ἀνθρώπων all that **feed on** human flesh, {{Refs|[prev. work] 50.}}
**III.2.b** **lay hands on,** χρημάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 913a; τῶν ἀλλοτρίων [prev. author] “R.” 360b, etc.}}
**III.3** **touch, affect,** ἄλγος οὐδὲν ἅπτεται νεκροῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 255, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 955; ἅπτει μου τοῖς λόγοις τῆς ψυχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 535a; τῆς ἐμῆς ἥψω φρενός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 916; ὥς μου χρησμὸς ἅ. φρενῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1237}}; **make an impression upon,** ἡμῶν{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 315.56 (Pessinus, 2nd c.BC).}}
**III.4** **grasp with the senses, perceive,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1550, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99e}}; **apprehend,** τῆς αἰτίας{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Respiratione” 472a3.}}
**III.5** **have intercourse with** a woman, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 84ca, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335b40, NT.1Cor.7.1; εὐνῆς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 946.}}
**III.6** **come up to, reach, overtake,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.43}}; **attain,** τῆς ἀληθείας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 65b; τοῦ τέλους [prev. author] “Smp.” 211b: in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, with _dative_, ἀγλαΐαις “P.” 10.28; στάλαισιν Ἡρακλείαις [prev. author] “I.” 4(3).12}}; but also with _genitive_, Ἡρακλέος σταλᾶν{{Refs|[prev. author] “O.” 3.44.}}
**III.7** **make use of, avail oneself of,** τῆς τύχης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 56.}}
