681:
** ἅπτω**,
_future_ ἅψω: _aorist_ ἧψα:—_passive_, _perfect_ ἧμμαι, _Ionic dialect_ ἅμμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86}}: _future_ ἅψομαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.379, ἁφθήσομαι}} (συν-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 3.311:}} —_middle_, see.below (compare ἑάφθη):—**fasten** or **bind to,** used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, once in}}_active_, ἅψας ἀμφοτέρωθεν.. ἔντερον οἰός (of a lyre-string) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.408}}; once in _middle_, ἁψαμένη βρόχον.. ἀφ᾽ ὑψηλοῖο μελάθρου **having fastened** the noose to the beam (to hang herself), {{Refs|11.278}}; so later ἅψεται ἀμφὶ βρόχον.. δείρᾳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 770; ἁψαμένη βρόχον αὐχένι 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1065}}:—_active_, βρόχους ἅ. κρεμαστούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1036; but βρόχῳ ἅ. δέρην [prev. author] “Hel.” 136, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 7.493 (Antip. Thess.).}}
**2** **join,** ἅ. χορόν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 307}}; πάλην τινὶ ἅ. **fasten** a contest in wrestling **on** one, **engage with** one, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 868}}: —_passive_, ἅπτεσθαι τὴν Μεγαρέων πόλιν καὶ Κορινθίων τοῖς τείχεσιν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280b14.}}
**II** more frequently in _middle_, ἅπτομαι, _future_ ἅψομαι, _aorist_ ἡψάμην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 317}}, with _perfect_ _passive_ ἧμμαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1010(Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260e}}:—**fasten oneself to, grasp,** c._genitive_, ἅψασθαι γούνων{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.512; χειρῶν 10.377; ἁψαμένη δὲ γενείου Ὀδυσσῆα προσέειπεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.473; ἅπτεσθαι νηῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.152; βρώμης δ᾽ οὐχ ἅπτεαι οὐδὲ ποτῆτο; 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.379, compare 4.60; ὡς δ᾽ ὅτε τίς τε κύων συὸς.. ἅπτηται κατόπισθε.. ἰσχία τε γλουτούς τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.339; ἅπτεσθαι τοῦ ἐπεόντος ἐπὶ τῶν δενδρέων καρποῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32; τῶν τύμβων ἁπτόμενοι [prev. author] 4.172}}; ἅπτεσθαί τινος, Latin _manus inicere alicui,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.137; οὔτ᾽ ἔθιγεν οὔθ᾽ ἥψαθ᾽ ἡμῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 617}}; τῶν σφυγμῶν **feel** the pulse, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.73}}: _metaphorically_, **take hold of, cleave to,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 967c.}}
**II.b** _absolutely_, τῶν μὲν γὰρ πάντων βέλε᾽ ἅπτεται for the spears of all the Trojans **reach their mark,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.631; ἀμφοτέρων βέλε᾽ ἥπτετο 8.67.}}
**II.c** ἅ. τῆς γῆς **land,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.48.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **engage in, undertake,** βουλευμάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 179; ἀγῶνος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 317}}; πολέμου **prosecute** it **vigorously,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.61; ἧπται τοῦ πράγματος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.155; ψυχὴ ἡμμένη φόνων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 108b, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 381; τῶν μεγίστων ἀσεβημάτων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.13.6; so ἅ. τῆς μουσικῆς καὶ φιλοσοφίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 411c}}; ἐπιτηδεύματος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 497e; γεωμετρίας [prev. author] “Plt.” 266a; τῆς θαλάττης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.24.7; ἅπτεσθαι λόγου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 662, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 283a (but ἅπτεσθαι τοῦ λόγου}} **attack, impugn** the argument of another, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 86d)}}; τούτων ἥψατο **touched on** these points, **handled** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97; ἅ. τῆς ζητήσεως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 320b34}}; but also, **touch on, treat superficially,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 694c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1227a1.}}
**III.b** _absolutely_, **begin, set to work,** ταῖς διανοίαις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 581.}}
**III.2** **fasten upon, attack,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.22, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1608, etc.; μόνον τῷ δακτύλῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 365; τῆς οὐραγίας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.34.12; especially with words, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.γ}}; of diseases, ἧπταί μου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1010, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.702; ἥψατο τῶν ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.48}}; ὅσα ἅπτεται ἀνθρώπων all that **feed on** human flesh, {{Refs|[prev. work] 50.}}
**III.2.b** **lay hands on,** χρημάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 913a; τῶν ἀλλοτρίων [prev. author] “R.” 360b, etc.}}
**III.3** **touch, affect,** ἄλγος οὐδὲν ἅπτεται νεκροῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 255, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 955; ἅπτει μου τοῖς λόγοις τῆς ψυχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 535a; τῆς ἐμῆς ἥψω φρενός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 916; ὥς μου χρησμὸς ἅ. φρενῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1237}}; **make an impression upon,** ἡμῶν{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 315.56 (Pessinus, 2nd c.BC).}}
**III.4** **grasp with the senses, perceive,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1550, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99e}}; **apprehend,** τῆς αἰτίας{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Respiratione” 472a3.}}
**III.5** **have intercourse with** a woman, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 84ca, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335b40, NT.1Cor.7.1; εὐνῆς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 946.}}
