683:
** ἀπωθέω**,
_future_ _infinitive_ ἀπωσέμεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.367}}: _aorist_ ἀπέωσα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.81, ἀπῶσα}} probably corrupt in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 479}}:—_middle_, _future_ ἀπεώσομαι{{LXX|LXX.2Ki.21.14}}: _aorist_ ἀπωσάμην{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), ἀπεωσάμην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, etc., ἀπωθησάμην 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.28 codices:}}_perfect_ ἀπῶσμαι· ἀπώθησα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}, _infinitive_ ἀπεῶσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ _participle_ ἀπωσμένος{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.33}} W.:—**thrust away, push back,** ὤϊξε πύλας καὶ ἀπῶσεν ὀχῆας{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.446, compare 21.537}}; ἀ. ἐπάλξεις **pushed** them **off** the wall, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23}}:—_middle_, **thrust away from oneself,** χερσὶν ἀπώσασθαι λίθον{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.305}}; ἀπώσατο ἦκα γέροντα **pushed** him gently **away,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.508; αἱ χεῖρες τὸ τόξον -οῦνταί τε καὶ προσέλκονται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 439b.}}
**2** **drive away,** ἠέρα μὲν σκέδασεν καὶ ἀπῶσεν ὀμίχλην [Ζεύς] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.649}}; of the wind, **beat from** one's course, Βορέης ἀπέωσε{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.81}} (so in _middle_, σφέας κεῖθεν ἀπώσατο ἲς ἀνέμοιο{{Refs|13.276).}}
**3** with _genitive_, εἴ κέ μιν οὐδοῦ ἀπώσομεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|22.76, compare 2.130; γῆς ἀπῶσαί [με] πατρίδος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 641}}:—_middle_, **thrust from oneself, drive away,** μνηστῆρας ἀπώσεαι ἐκ μεγάροιο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.270}}:—_passive_, **to be expelled,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.173; ἀπωθοῦμαι δόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 450; γῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 670.}}
**4** **thrust aside, spurn,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 446, +others}}:—_passive_, τὸν δῆμον πρότερον ἀπωσμένον **pushed aside,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.69.}}
**5** **repel, drive back,** in _middle_, Τρῶας ἀπώσασθαι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.206; νεῖκος ἀπωσαμένους 12.276; ἀπώσασθαι κακὰ νηῶν 15.503; νηῶν μὲν ἀπωσάμενοι δήϊον πῦρ 16.301; πένθος 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 9.10; νοῦσον “Anthologia Graeca” 6.190 (1st c.AD: Gaetulicus, Cn. Lentulus Epigrammaticus)}}:— also in Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.4.6, etc.: with double _accusative_, τὴν ναυμαχίαν ἀπεωσάμεθα Κορινθίους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32.}}
**6** in _middle_, **reject,** τὸ ἀργύριον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199; τὸν αὐλόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 216(Lyric poetry); φιλότητα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1122(Lyric poetry); τὰς σπονδάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.22; τὰ ἐξ ἀδικίας κέρδη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366a}}; ἀ. πόνους **decline** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 789}}; τὴν δουλοσύνην **shake off** slavery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95}}; ὕπνον **shake off** sleep, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 571c}}: _absolutely_, **refuse,** ποιήσω κοὐκ ἀπώσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1249.}}

684:
** ἀπώλεια**, ἡ,
**destruction,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1120a2, etc.: _plural_, [prev. author] “Mete.” 351b11.}}
**II** **loss,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 952b26}}; opposed to τήρησις, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.59.5 Schweigh., compare “BGU” 1058.35, +others (1st c.BC)}}; τῶν χρόνων ἀ.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 1.}}
**II.2** **perdition,** {{NT|NT.Rom.9.22, NT.2Thes.2.3.}}
**II.3** **thing lost,** {{LXX|LXX.Lev.6.3 (5.22).}}

685:
** ἀρά**,
_Ionic dialect_ ἀρή, ἡ, **prayer,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.378, 598, 23.199, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 726, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).43}}; ἀρὴν ἐποιήσαντο παῖδα γενέσθαι Ἀρίστωνι offered **prayers** that a child should be born, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.63.}}
**2** **vow,** {{Refs|Inscription Cyprian dialect 83, 147H.}}
**3** **curse, imprecation,** ἐξ ἀρέων μητρὸς.. ἥ ῥα θεοῖσι πόλλ᾽ ἀχέουσ᾽ ἠρᾶτο{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.566}}; frequently in Trag., mostly in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 910, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 295}}; ἀρὰς ἀρᾶσθαι,προστιθέναι, ἐξανιέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 67, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 952, 154(Lyric poetry), 1375; ἐπεύχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 119e; θέσθαι ἐπί τινας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 13}}: also in _singular_, πατρὸς δ᾽ ἀ. κρανθήσεται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 910, compare “Ag.” 457 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἡ τοῦ νόμου ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 871b; ἀρᾷ.. ἔνοχος ἔστω [prev. work] 742b}}, etc.: in _plural_, **imprecations,** frequently in Inscrr. on those who shall mutilate or remove them, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Magn.” 105.53 (2nd c.BC), “IG” 3.1417ff}}
**II** Ἀρά personified as the goddess of destruction and revenge, ὦ πότνι᾽ Ἀρὰ σεμναί τε θεῶν παῖδες Ἐρινύες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” III; δεινόπους Ἀρά [prev. author] “OT” 418; but in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 417 the Erinyes say that Ἀραί}} is their own name γῆς ὑπα; Ἀρά τ᾽ Ἐρινὺς πατρὸς ἡ μεγασθενής{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 70; Ἀρᾶς ἱερόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 575. (Hence the Verb ἀράομαι.)}} [_Epic dialect_ always ᾱρ, _Attic dialect_ always ᾰρ.] (From ἀρϝᾱ, compare κάταρ ϝος.)

686:
** ἄρᾰ**,
_Epic dialect_ ῥά (which is enclitic and used after monosyllables, ἦ, ὅς, γάρ, etc., or words ending in a vowel or diphthong, e.g. ἐπεί), before a consonant ἄρ (perhaps cf. Lithuanian _i[rtilde]_ 'and'): expressing consequence, **then,** or mere succession, **there and then,** and in many derived uses.
**A** EARLIER USAGE: to denote,
**A.I** immediate transition, **there and then, straightway,** ὣς φάτο βῆ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ Ὄνειρος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.16}}: after a _participle_, ὣς εἰπὼν κατ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἕζετο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.68, +others; πυθόμενος.. εἶπε ἄ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134, compare 9.9; ἐρωτώσης τῆς μητρὸς ἀπεκρίνατο ἄρα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.2; with other Particles, δέ, ἦ, ὡς, etc., compare ὁ δὲ Ἀστυάγης ἄ. εἶπεν [prev. work] 4.10: also after _adverbs_ of Time, τότε δή ῥα, τῆμος ἄρα, etc.}}; οὕτως ἄρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 259b}}; often in apodosi, as αὐτὰρ ἐπεὶ δὴ θηήσατο.. αὐτίκ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἤλυθεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.77; repeated τὼ μὲν ἄρ᾽.. κεῖντο ἡ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽.. ἀγόρευε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.426}}: in enumerations, e. g. in Homer's catalogue, **then, next,** οἱ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ Ἀθήνας εἶχον{{Refs|2.546}}; so in genealogies, Σίσυφος.. ὁ δ᾽ ἄ. Γλαῦκον τέκεθ᾽ υἱόν{{Refs|6.154.}}
**A.I.2** to draw attention, **mark you!** τὸν τρεῖς μὲν ἐπιρρήσσεσκον.. τῶν ἄλλων Ἀχιλεὺς δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἐπιρρήσσεσκε καὶ οἶος{{Refs|24.456; with _imperative_, ἀλλ᾽ ἄγε δὴ κατ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἕζευ 24.522}}: to point a moral or general statement, φευγόντων δ᾽ οὔτ᾽ ἂρ κλέος ὄρνυται οὔτε τις ἀλκή{{Refs|5.532.}}
**A.II** connexion, such as,
**A.II.1** that of **antecedent** and **consequent,** οἰνοχόει.. ἄσβεστος δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἐνῶρτο γέλως{{Refs|1.599, compare 24.507; τοὔνεκ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἄλγε᾽ ἔδωκε 1.96; frequently with οὕνεκα in protasi, 7.140, +others}}: also in questions, τίς τ᾽ ἄρ τῶν ὄχ᾽ ἄριστος ἔη; who **then** (say you) was.. ? {{Refs|2.761: with _demonstrative_ Pronoun in recapitulation, ἀλλ᾽ υἱὸν Πριάμοιο.. τόν ῥ᾽ Ὀδυσεὺς βάλε}}**he it was, whom**.., {{Refs|4.501: frequently in such phrases as ὣς ἄρα φωνήσας 2.35, +others; ὣς ἄρ᾽ ἔφη 1.584, +others; ἦ ῥα 3.355, +others}}, thus, **then** he spoke.—This usage is universal in Greek.
**A.II.2** explanation of that which precedes, χωόμενον κατὰ θυμὸν ἐϋζώνοιο γυναικὸς τήν ῥα.. ἀπηύρων whom (**and for this cause** he was angry) they had taken away, {{Refs|1.429}}; εἰ μὴ ὑπερφίαλον ἔπος ἔκβαλε.. φῆ ῥ᾽ ἀέκητι θεῶν φυγέειν **for** he said, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.504: frequently with οὕνεκα}}; so with relatives, ἐκ δ᾽ ἔθορε κλῆρος ὃν ἄρ᾽ ἤθελον αὐτοί the **very** one which.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.182.}}
**B** LATER USAGE, always with inferential force: 1. in drawing conclusions (more subjective than οὖν), ἄριστον ἄ. ἡ εὐδαιμονία{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1099a24; δῆλον ἄ. [prev. author] “Pol.” 1295b33; in pseudo-syllogistic conclusions, [prev. author] “SE” 174b11, “Rh.” 1401a3, +others}}: especially by way of informal inference, **as it seems,** οὐκ ἄ. σοί γε πατὴρ ἦν Πηλεύς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.33; οὐδ᾽ ἄ. πως ἦν ἐν πάντεσσ᾽ ἔργοισι δαήμονα φῶτα γενέσθαι 23.670; μάτην ἄρ᾽, ὡς ἔοικεν, ἥκομεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 772}}; οὕτω κοινόν τι ἄ. χαρᾷ καὶ λύπῃ δάκρυά ἐστιν **so true is it that**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.32; πολὺ γὰρ ἀμείνων ἄ. ὁ τοῦ ἀδίκου ἢ ὁ τοῦ δικαίου βίος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 358c; ἦν ἄ. πυρός γ᾽ ἕτερα.. θερμότερα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 382; ὦ τλῆμον ἀρετή, λόγος ἄρ᾽ ἦσθα “Tragica Adespota” 374}}; so in announcing the discovery or correction of an error, as οὐκ ἐννενοήκαμεν ὅτι εἰσὶν ἄ... {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 375d; φαίνεται πρὸ ποδῶν ἡμῖν κυλινδεῖσθαι καὶ οὐχ ἑωρῶμεν ἄρ᾽ αὐτό [prev. work] 432d}}; εἰκότως ἄ. οὐκ ἐγίγνετο· ὡς γὰρ ἐγὼ νῦν πυνθάνομαι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.3.}}
**B.2** in questions, expressing the anxiety of the questioner, τίς ἄ. ῥύσετα; **who is there** to save ? {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 92}}; so in exclamations to heighten the expression of emotion, οἵαν ἄρ᾽ ἥβην.. ἀπώλεσεν **what** a band of youth was that.. ! {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 733}}; so ὡς ἄρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 472, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 577; τί μ᾽ ἄ. τί μ᾽ ὀλέκει; [prev. author] “Ant.” 1285; τί οὖν.. ἄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.2}}; πῶς ἄ.; οὕτως ἄ., etc.; ἄ. alone, ἔζης ἄ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 686}}: especially in ironical comments, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 476, 1371, etc.}}
**B.3** epexegetic, **namely,** ἐρῶ, ὡς ἄ... {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, compare 156e.}}
**B.4** for τοι ἄρα, τἄρα, see entry τοι {{Refs|11.2.}}
**B.5** εἰ (or ἐάν) μὴ ἄ. unless **perhaps,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.4}}; separated from εἰ μή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.20; with irony, εἰ μὴ ἄ. ἡ τῆς ἀρετῆς ἐπιμέλεια διαφθορά ἐστιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.8.}}
**B.6** in hypothetical clauses, to indicate the improbability of the supposition, ἢν ἄ. ποτὲ κατὰ γῆν βιασθῶσιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93, etc.}}; or simply, **perhaps** (sometimes separated from εἰ), εἴ τις οὖν ὑμῶν ἄ... ὑπελάμβανεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.8; εἴ τις ἰδίᾳ τινὰ δεδιὼς ἄ. ἀπρόθυμός ἐστιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86.}}
**C** IN CRASIS, frequently τἄρα, μεντἄρα, οὐτἄρα: also δήξομἄρα for δήξομαι ἄ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325}}; οἰμώξετἄρα, κλαύσἄρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 248, “Pax” 532: also in Trag., 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hyps.fragment” 34.86.}}
**D** ἄρα never stands first in the sentence in Classical Greek {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 851a22 is corrupt)}}, but is found at the beginning of an apodosis in {{NT|NT.Matt.12.28, NT.Rom.10.17}}, and first in a sentence, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.11.48, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 305.20}}; in conclusion of syllogism, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.11.31.}}

687:
** ἆρᾰ**,
_interrogative_ Particle, implying **anxiety** or **impatience,** = _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry ἦ ῥα:
**1** alone, it simply marks the question, the nature of which is determined by the context: e.g. in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.44}} a **negative** answer is implied in the question ἆρ᾽ ἂν οἴεσθε..; but an **affirmative** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.4}} ἆ. βέβληκα δὶς ἐφεξῆ; compare ἆρ᾽ εὐτυχεῖς.. ἢ δυστυχεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 424.—To make it plainly _negative_, we have ἆ. μή..; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 208, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 64c}}; and to make it plainly affirmative, ἆρ᾽ ο; ἆρ᾽ οὐχ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 791, “OT” 540}}; ἆρ᾽ οὐχ οὕτω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 11d.}}
**2** ἆ. οὖ; is used to draw an affirmative inference, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 477a, “La.” 190b}}; also when a _negative_ answer is expected, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 159b}}; with a _negative_, ἆρ᾽ οὖν οὐ..; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 263a, etc.}}
**3** in ἆρά γε, each Particle retains its force, γε serving to make the question more definite, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 546, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.4, etc.}}
**4** less frequently with τίς _interrogative_, τίνος ποτ᾽ ἆρ᾽ ἔπραξε χειρί{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 905}}; τίδ᾽ ἆρ᾽ ἐγὼ σ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1228; τίς ἆρ᾽ ὁ φεύγω; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 893}}; with ἤν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 118.}}
**5** in indirect questions, σκεψώμεθα τοῦτο ἆ... {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 70e, compare “R.” 526c, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 204b3, etc.}}
**II** in Poets sometimes like{ἄρα}, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 86, 89, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 3; τοιοῖσδε χρησμοῖς ἆ. χρὴ πεποιθένα; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 297, compare 435; τῷ δὲ ξιφήρης ἆρ᾽ ὑφειστήκει λόχος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1114: in exclamations, βραδεῖαν ἡμᾶς ἆρ᾽ ὁ τήνδε τὴν ὁδὸν πέμπων ἔπεμψεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 738; ὀδυνηρὸς ἆρ᾽ ὁ πλοῦτος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 566, compare “El.” 1229, “Hipp.” 1086; ἦ δεινὸν ἆρ᾽ ἦν [prev. author] “fragment” 931; ἔμελλόν σ᾽ ἆ. κινήσειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1301, compare “Ach.” 347.}}
**B** In Prose, ἆ. almost always stands first in the sentence, but{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 467e}}; καὶ ὑπὲρ τούτων ἆ... {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.61c: in Poetry greater licence is taken, see above 1.4, 11.}}

691:
** ἀργέω**,
_future_ -ήσω: _aorist_ ἤργησα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 698.4 (2nd c.AD)}}: _perfect_ ἤργηκα{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1160.14 (3rd-4th c.AD)}}: (ἀργός, ἀεργός):—**to be unemployed, do nothing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 23, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 625, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 426a, etc.}}; **keep Sabbath,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.2Mac.5.25; ἀ. τὴν ἑβδόμην 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 7.3.3}}; οἱ ἀργοῦντες **the idle,** {{Refs|Trag.Adesp. 527}}; γῆ ἀργοῦσα **lying fallow,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.11, “PFlor.” 262.9 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἀργῆσαν ἤμυσε στέγος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 864; φησὶν ἀργῆσαι τὸ ἐργαστήριον}}**is out of work,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.19}}; of the senses, **to be at rest,** νυκτὸς τῆς ὄψεως ἀργούσης{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 903a21, compare “Somn.Vig.” 455a30}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, ἀργήσει.. τῆς αὑτοῦ δημιουργίας **will be unoccupied in** his own work, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371c.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be left undone,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.3}}; **to be fruitless,** ἡ σκέψις ἂν ἀργοῖτο{{Refs|[prev. author] “Hier.” 9.9.}}

692:
** ἀργός**, όν,
**B**later ή, όν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1167a11, “Mete.” 352a13, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 27, 2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 12.11, etc.: (_contraction_ from ἀεργός)}}:—properly **not working the ground,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.6}}; **idle, lazy,** opposed to ἐργάτις, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 97, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 53, etc.; γαστέρες ἀ. 6th c.BC: Epimenides Philosophus 1; ἀ. ἐπιθυμίαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 572e}}; ἀ. τὴν διάνοιαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 458a; τὸ πρὸς ἅπαν ξυνετὸν ἐπὶ πᾶν ἀ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; ἂν ἀ. ᾖ if he have **no trade,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 123.3; πότερον ἀνθρώπου οὐδέν ἐστιν [ἔργον] ἀλλ᾽ ἀργὸν πέφυκε; 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1097b30}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, **idle at** a thing, **free from** it, τῶν οἴκοθεν **from** domestic toils, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1000; πόνων σφοδρῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 835d; γυναῖκας ἀργοὺς ταλασίας [prev. work] 806a}}; ἀ. αἰσχρῶν **slow to** evil, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 411; ἀργότεραι ἐς τὸ δρᾶν τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.67; ἀ. περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 966d.}}
**B.2** of things, δόρυ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1387}}; of money, **lying idle, yielding no return,** opposed to ἐνεργός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.7}} and 20; of land, **lying fallow,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.132, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.19, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.12.2}}; opposed to πεφυτευμένος, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IG” 7.2226 B (Thisbe, 3rd c.AD)}}; διατριβὴ ἀ. **in which nothing is done, fruitless,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1498 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.44; χρόνον ἀργὸν διάγειν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 31}}. _adverb_ ἀργῶς, ἐπιμέλεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.7; ἔχειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.3: _comparative_ and _superlative_ ἀργότερον, -ότατα, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 15.6 and 1.}}
**B.2.b** ἀ. λόγος, name of a **sophism,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.277, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.574e.}}
**B.II** _passive_,**unwrought,** ἁρμός, κυμάτιον, {{Refs|“IG” 1.322b23,59; πυροὶ ἀ.}}**unprepared for eating,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 13; ἄργυρος 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.12.3}}; βύρσαι **undressed** hides, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus [same place]}}; **unpolished,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 27.}}
**B.II.2** **not done, left undone,** κοὐκ ἦν ἔτ᾽ οὐδὲν ἀ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1605; ἓν δ᾽ ἐστὶν ἡμῖν ἀ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 766}}; οὐκ ἐν ἀργοῖς not among **things neglected,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 287; τὰ μὲν προβέβηκεν ἀμήχανόν ἐστι γενέσθαι ἀργά 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 584.}}
**B.II.3** **unattempted,** μάχη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a.}}
**B.II.4** Astrology texts, τόπος ἀ., name of the {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|8th of the 12 'houses', 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 128, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “M.” 4; πλανήτης 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.3.3; ζῴδιον 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.15.}}

693:
** ἀργύρεος** [ῠ], α, ον,
_contraction_ ἀργῠροῦς, ᾶ, οῦν: **of silver,** of the bow of Apollo, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.49, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.32; κρήτηρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.741, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.615, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 184; τάλαρον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.125; λάρναξ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.412; ἀσάμινθοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.128, etc.; γένος 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 144, etc.; ἀ. πλοῦτος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 801d.}}
**2** **silver-plated,** κλῖναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.82.}}
**II** as _substantive_, ἀργυροῦς, ὁ, **silver coin,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Zech.11.12, +others, “SIG” 731.20 (Tomi, 1st c.BC), 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Mens.” 60.1, +others}}

694:
** ἀργύριον** [ῠ], τό,
**small coin, piece of money,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 262, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.16, etc.: _plural_ (see. 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.89), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 600, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 155, X.[same place]: then}}
**2** collectively, **money,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 156, 158, +others}}; ἀ. ῥητόν a fixed **sum,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.70}}; εἰς ἀ. λογισθέντα calculated in **our money,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.33}}; ἀ. καθαρόν 'hard **cash**', {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.36: in Comedy texts with _Article_, τἀργύριον}} **the money, the cash,** δανείζεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 756}}; ἀπαιτεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1247; κατατιθέναι 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 124.14, etc.; so τἀ. καταβάλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.27, etc.}}
**II** ={ἄργυρος}, **silver,** πεντηκοσίας μνέας ἀργυρίου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13}}; ἀ. ἐπίσημον and ἄσημον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13; χρυσίον καὶ ἀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.122e; ἀργυρίου ἄνθος}}**lead oxide,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 33.}}

695:
** ἀργῠρο-κόπος**, ὁ,
(κόπτω) **coiner,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 5.}}
**II** **silversmith,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.830e, “SIG” 1263 (from Smyrna), 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.102, 103, LXX.Judg.17.4, NT.Act.19.24, “PHaw.” 68.3 (1st c.AD).}}

696:
** ἄργῠρος**, ὁ,
(ἀργός A) **white metal,** i.e. **silver,** ἐξ Ἀλύβης ὅθεν ἀργύρου ἐστὶ γενέθλη{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.857; so πηγὴ ἀργύρου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 238, etc.; ἄ. κοῖλος}}**silver plate,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 283a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1350b23, etc.}}
**2** ἄ. χυτός **quicksilver,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “de An.” 406b19, “Mete.” 385b4, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 60.}}
**II** ={ἀργύριον}, **silver-money,** generally, **money,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 935; ἐπ᾽ ἀργύρῳ γε τὴν ψυχὴν προδούς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 322}}; εἴ τι μὴ ξὺν ἀργύρῳ ἐπράσσετ᾽ by **bribery,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 124}}; in later Prose, coupled with χρυσός, {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Matt.10.9, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.3.}}
**III** ={λινόζωστος ἄρρην}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.189.}}

697:
Related to: ** Ἄρειος** [ᾰ], ον,
also α, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 413 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Ionic dialect_ Ἀρήϊος, η, ο; _Aeolic dialect_ Ἀρεύϊος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 1a.10}}: (Ἄρης):— **devoted to Ares, warlike, martial**; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, mostly of warriors, Μενέλαος Ἀρήιος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.339, +others; Ἀρήϊοι υἷες Ἀχαιῶν 11.800, +others}}; of arms, Ἀρήϊα τεύχεα δύω{{Refs|6.340, compare 10.407}}; the _Attic dialect_ form only in the phrase τεῖχος Ἄρειον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.407, +others; also in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἀρήιοι ἀγῶνες}} conflicts **in real war,** opposed to γυμνικοί,{{Refs|9.33; ὅπλον ἀ. 4.23}}; of a man, as _substantive_, **warrior,**{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6.98; Ἀθηνᾶ Ἀρεία “OGI” 229.70 (from Smyrna), compare “IG” 12(5).913 (from Tenos): _comparative_ ἀρειότερος (which see) 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 548 is probably to be read instead of med from ἀρείων, as χερειότερος from χερείων.}}
**2** Ἄρειος (also Ἄρεος, Ἄρηος), ὁ (i.e. μήν), name of month in Thessaly, {{Refs|“GDI” 1449, etc.}}
**3** Ἄρειον πεδίον, = **Campus Martius,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.59, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 53}} (also name of a plain in Thrace, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.10.7)}}; τῶν τελῶν τὸ καλούμενον Ἄρειον, = **legio Martia,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.45}}; Ἄρεια, τά, = **ludi Martiales,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 56.46.}}
**4** Ἄρειον, τό, ={ξιφίον}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.20}}; ={ἰσάτις}, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.184.}}
**II** ={Ἄριος, Μάγοι καὶ πᾶν τὸ Ἄρειον γένος} {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 125.}}
**A Ἄρειος πάγος** [πᾰ], ὁ, **the hill of Ares,** at Athens, Ἀρήϊος π.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.52, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 685, 690, etc.; ἡ βουλὴ ἡ ἐξ Ἀρείου πάγου “IG” 1.38a (probably), 2.476.59, +others, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.133, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.12, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 4.4; ἡ ἐν Ἀ. πάγῳ βουλή 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.157, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.81, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1273b39; βουλή Ἀρεία “IG” 3.824}}; εἰς τὸν Ἄ. πάγον ἀναβῆναι become a member of the court, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.37, 12.154; ἐν Ἀ. πάγῳ δοῦναι δίκην 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1398b26; ψευδομαρτύρια τὰ ἐξ Ἀ. πάγου [prev. author] “Ath.” 59.6.}}—The _compound_ Ἀρειόπᾰγος only in a late _Attic dialect_ Inscription, {{Refs|“IG” 3.1005}}; but we find the noun Ἀρεοπᾰγίτης (Ἀρευ- {{Refs|[prev. work]2.839.7) [ῑ]}} ου, ὁ, **Areopagite,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.81, “IG” 3.746, compare 635, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 3.6, etc.: proverbial, Ἀρεοπαγίτου σιωπηλότερος}} 'as silent as the grave', {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 21.263a; στεγανώτερος 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.13}}:—_adjective_ Ἀρεοπᾰγῖτις, βουλή{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 41.2, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.3}}; Ἀρεοπᾰγῑτικός, ή, όν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7 at Title Page, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.1.8.}}

